window.CORE_CONCEPTS = [{"id":"ACC-3ST","t":"Three financial statements","cat":"Accounting","sub":"Three-statement linking","f":5,"d":"The income statement measures profitability over a period, the cash flow statement reconciles net income to cash movement, and the balance sheet shows assets, liabilities and equity at a point in time.","formula":"Assets = Liabilities + Equity","why":"Interview questions test whether you can trace an operating or financing event through profit, cash and balance-sheet accounts without double counting.","caveat":"Timing, classification and non-cash items can make accounting earnings diverge from cash; IFRS and US GAAP may classify some items differently.","trap_q":"A company has positive net income but falling cash. Is that impossible?","trap_a":"No. Working-capital investment, capex, debt repayment or other cash uses can reduce cash despite positive earnings.","es":"Los tres estados se conectan: la utilidad neta alimenta el cash flow; el cambio neto de caja actualiza el balance; y retained earnings refleja la utilidad menos dividendos.","related":["Net income","Cash flow statement","Balance sheet"]},{"id":"ACC-REV","t":"Revenue recognition","cat":"Accounting","sub":"Income statement","f":4,"d":"Revenue is recognised when the company satisfies its performance obligation, not simply when cash is received.","formula":"Revenue recognised ≠ cash collected","why":"It determines the top line, margins, receivables or deferred revenue and can materially affect earnings quality.","caveat":"Aggressive assumptions on timing, variable consideration or collectability can make reported growth look stronger than underlying economics.","trap_q":"Cash arrives before the service is delivered. Revenue now?","trap_a":"Usually no. Cash increases and a deferred-revenue liability is recorded until the performance obligation is satisfied.","es":"Ingresos y caja no son lo mismo: cobrar antes suele crear deferred revenue; vender a crédito suele crear accounts receivable.","related":["Accounts receivable","Deferred revenue","Earnings quality"]},{"id":"ACC-COGS","t":"COGS","cat":"Accounting","sub":"Income statement","f":4,"d":"Cost of goods sold is the direct cost attributable to goods or services sold during the period.","formula":"Gross profit = Revenue − COGS","why":"COGS drives gross margin and is central to understanding unit economics, pricing power and cost inflation.","caveat":"Classification differs by business model; comparing gross margin across sectors without normalising cost classification can mislead.","trap_q":"A manufacturer capitalises more overhead into inventory. What happens initially?","trap_a":"Current COGS may be lower and inventory higher, boosting gross profit initially; the cost is recognised later when inventory is sold.","es":"COGS son los costos directamente ligados a lo vendido; mover costos entre COGS y opex puede cambiar márgenes sin cambiar necesariamente la economía total.","related":["Gross margin","Inventory","Operating expenses"]},{"id":"ACC-EBITDA","t":"EBITDA","cat":"Accounting","sub":"Income statement","f":5,"d":"EBITDA is earnings before interest, taxes, depreciation and amortisation; it approximates operating earnings before capital structure, tax and non-cash D&A.","formula":"EBITDA = EBIT + D&A","why":"It is widely used for enterprise-value multiples and leverage because it is before interest and is more comparable across capital structures than net income.","caveat":"It is not cash flow: it ignores capex, working capital, taxes and often other recurring cash needs.","trap_q":"Two companies have identical EBITDA but very different free cash flow. How?","trap_a":"Different capex intensity, working-capital needs, cash taxes, restructuring costs or lease economics can produce very different cash conversion.","es":"EBITDA sirve para aproximar desempeño operativo antes de estructura financiera, pero no equivale a caja.","related":["EBIT","Free cash flow","EV/EBITDA"]},{"id":"ACC-DA","t":"Depreciation & amortisation","cat":"Accounting","sub":"Income statement","f":5,"d":"D&A allocates the cost of tangible and intangible assets over their useful lives and is generally non-cash in the current period.","formula":"EBIT = EBITDA − D&A","why":"D&A links past investment and acquisition accounting to current earnings, taxes and asset balances.","caveat":"It is non-cash today but often represents consumption of assets that eventually require replacement investment.","trap_q":"Depreciation rises by $10; tax rate is 25%. Walk through the statements.","trap_a":"EBIT and pre-tax income fall $10, taxes fall $2.5, net income falls $7.5; add back $10 in CFO, so cash rises $2.5; PP&E falls $10 and retained earnings falls $7.5, balancing with the $2.5 cash increase.","es":"Si D&A sube, baja la utilidad y los impuestos; se agrega de vuelta en CFO por ser no-cash, mientras el activo neto disminuye.","related":["PP&E","CapEx","Deferred taxes"]},{"id":"ACC-CAPEX","t":"Capital expenditure","cat":"Accounting","sub":"Cash flow statement","f":5,"d":"CapEx is cash spent to acquire or improve long-lived operating assets.","formula":"Ending PP&E ≈ Beginning PP&E + CapEx − Depreciation ± disposals/FX","why":"CapEx is a key driver of asset growth, free cash flow and valuation, especially in capital-intensive sectors.","caveat":"Reported CapEx may mix maintenance and growth spending; management rarely discloses the split precisely.","trap_q":"CapEx increases by $100 with no immediate depreciation. What happens?","trap_a":"CFO is unchanged, CFI falls $100, cash falls $100, PP&E rises $100; there is no immediate income-statement impact if the spend is capitalised.","es":"CapEx sale por investing cash flow y aumenta PP&E; no golpea inmediatamente el P&L si se capitaliza.","related":["PP&E","Free cash flow","Capitalising vs expensing"]},{"id":"ACC-NWC","t":"Net working capital","cat":"Accounting","sub":"Working capital","f":5,"d":"Operating net working capital is typically operating current assets minus operating current liabilities, often excluding cash and debt.","formula":"NWC ≈ AR + Inventory + other operating CA − AP − accrued/deferred operating CL","why":"Changes in NWC convert accounting revenue and expenses into cash timing and are crucial in forecasting free cash flow.","caveat":"Definitions vary by model and transaction; including cash or debt can distort operating working capital.","trap_q":"Why is an increase in NWC usually a use of cash?","trap_a":"Because more cash is tied up in receivables/inventory or fewer supplier/customer financing liabilities are available; the cash has not yet been realised.","es":"Un aumento de NWC normalmente consume caja porque más recursos quedan atrapados en cuentas por cobrar o inventario, neto de financiación operativa.","related":["Accounts receivable","Inventory","Accounts payable"]},{"id":"ACC-AR","t":"Accounts receivable","cat":"Accounting","sub":"Working capital","f":4,"d":"AR is revenue recognised but not yet collected in cash.","formula":"DSO ≈ Average AR / Revenue × days","why":"AR growth can reveal customer terms, collections, revenue quality and working-capital intensity.","caveat":"Seasonality, acquisitions and rapid growth can raise AR without necessarily implying deteriorating collections.","trap_q":"DSO jumps from 35 to 60 days. Name plausible explanations.","trap_a":"Slower collections, weaker customer credit, longer terms, mix shift, aggressive revenue recognition, seasonality or growth concentrated late in the period.","es":"Accounts receivable es venta ya reconocida pero aún no cobrada; DSO ayuda a evaluar cuánto tarda en convertirse en caja.","related":["DSO","Revenue recognition","Cash conversion cycle"]},{"id":"ACC-INV","t":"Inventory","cat":"Accounting","sub":"Working capital","f":4,"d":"Inventory is product or inputs held for production or sale and is an operating current asset.","formula":"DIO ≈ Average inventory / COGS × days","why":"Inventory affects cash conversion, gross margin risk and operational efficiency.","caveat":"Higher inventory can support growth or signal weak demand/obsolescence; interpretation depends on the reason.","trap_q":"Inventory is written down by $20. What is the broad effect?","trap_a":"An expense lowers pre-tax income; inventory falls. The non-cash write-down is generally added back in CFO initially, with tax effects depending on deductibility.","es":"Inventario inmoviliza caja; un write-down reduce el activo y la utilidad, aunque el cargo puede ser no-cash en ese momento.","related":["DIO","COGS","Write-down"]},{"id":"ACC-AP","t":"Accounts payable","cat":"Accounting","sub":"Working capital","f":4,"d":"AP is money owed to suppliers for goods or services already received.","formula":"DPO ≈ Average AP / COGS × days","why":"AP provides operating financing and affects the cash conversion cycle.","caveat":"Extending payables can improve short-term cash but may strain suppliers or signal liquidity pressure.","trap_q":"AP rises by $20 because payment is delayed. Cash effect?","trap_a":"CFO is $20 higher than it otherwise would be because the company has not yet paid the supplier; AP on the balance sheet rises $20.","es":"Accounts payable funciona como financiación operativa: retrasar el pago conserva caja, aunque no crea valor por sí solo.","related":["DPO","Cash conversion cycle","Supplier financing"]},{"id":"ACC-DEFREV","t":"Deferred revenue","cat":"Accounting","sub":"Working capital","f":5,"d":"Deferred revenue is cash collected before the related revenue is recognised; it is a liability until performance obligations are satisfied.","formula":"Cash received before revenue → Cash ↑ and Deferred revenue ↑","why":"It often provides favourable customer financing and can make working capital structurally negative in subscription or prepaid models.","caveat":"It is not debt in the conventional sense, but the company still owes future goods or services.","trap_q":"Deferred revenue rises by $50 with no service delivered yet. P&L impact?","trap_a":"No immediate revenue or profit; cash rises $50 and the deferred-revenue liability rises $50, increasing CFO under indirect presentation.","es":"Deferred revenue es caja recibida antes de reconocer el ingreso; es una obligación de entregar el producto o servicio.","related":["Revenue recognition","Negative working capital","Subscription models"]},{"id":"ACC-GW","t":"Goodwill","cat":"Accounting","sub":"M&A accounting","f":5,"d":"Goodwill is the residual purchase-accounting asset created when consideration plus relevant interests exceeds the fair value of identifiable net assets acquired.","formula":"Goodwill ≈ Purchase consideration + NCI + prior interest − FV identifiable net assets","why":"It captures expected synergies, assembled workforce and other value not separately recognised as identifiable assets.","caveat":"Goodwill is not a separately saleable asset and can be impaired if expected economics deteriorate; accounting differs on amortisation for some private-company regimes.","trap_q":"Can goodwill be negative?","trap_a":"In a bargain purchase the residual can be negative before reassessment; after confirming valuations, the gain is generally recognised rather than carrying negative goodwill under modern standards.","es":"Goodwill es el residuo del precio de compra sobre los activos netos identificables a valor razonable.","related":["Purchase price allocation","Impairment","Intangibles"]},{"id":"ACC-INTANG","t":"Identifiable intangible assets","cat":"Accounting","sub":"M&A accounting","f":4,"d":"Identifiable intangibles are non-physical assets such as customer relationships, brands, patents or technology that can be separately recognised in purchase accounting.","formula":"Purchase accounting may create intangible assets at fair value","why":"They reduce residual goodwill and may create future amortisation that affects post-deal earnings.","caveat":"Valuation is assumption-heavy and useful lives can materially change amortisation expense.","trap_q":"A buyer allocates more purchase price to finite-lived intangibles instead of goodwill. EPS impact?","trap_a":"All else equal, future amortisation expense is higher, reducing accounting earnings, although cash flow may be less affected before taxes.","es":"Más asignación a intangibles amortizables implica menos goodwill y normalmente más amortización futura.","related":["Goodwill","PPA","Amortisation"]},{"id":"ACC-IMP","t":"Impairment","cat":"Accounting","sub":"Non-cash charges","f":4,"d":"An impairment reduces an asset’s carrying value when its recoverable or fair value has fallen below the recorded amount under the applicable accounting rules.","formula":"Asset carrying value ↓; impairment expense ↑","why":"It can reveal that prior investment or acquisition assumptions have deteriorated and affects book value and sometimes covenants.","caveat":"Often non-cash at recognition and methodologies differ across asset classes and accounting standards.","trap_q":"Does a goodwill impairment reduce enterprise value mechanically?","trap_a":"Not directly by the accounting entry alone; it reduces book equity and signals weaker economics, which may influence market value, but EV is market-based.","es":"Un impairment reduce el valor contable del activo y la utilidad, pero no destruye caja en el momento del cargo.","related":["Goodwill","Book value","Non-cash charges"]},{"id":"ACC-DTA","t":"Deferred tax asset","cat":"Accounting","sub":"Deferred taxes","f":4,"d":"A DTA represents future tax benefits from deductible temporary differences or tax attributes such as loss carryforwards, subject to recognition rules.","formula":"DTA arises when tax deductions are expected later than book expense, or from usable tax losses","why":"DTAs affect purchase accounting, valuation of tax attributes and the bridge between book and cash taxes.","caveat":"Realisation may depend on future taxable income; valuation allowances can reduce recognised DTAs.","trap_q":"A company has huge NOLs. Are they worth face value?","trap_a":"No. Value depends on tax rates, timing, future taxable income and legal limitations on use; the present value can be far below the nominal loss balance.","es":"Un DTA representa ahorro fiscal futuro potencial; su valor depende de poder generar renta imponible y utilizarlo legalmente.","related":["NOLs","Cash taxes","Valuation allowance"]},{"id":"ACC-DTL","t":"Deferred tax liability","cat":"Accounting","sub":"Deferred taxes","f":4,"d":"A DTL represents taxes expected to be paid in the future because book carrying values and tax bases differ.","formula":"DTL often arises when tax depreciation is faster than book depreciation or in asset write-ups","why":"DTLs are important in PPA because fair-value write-ups can create taxable temporary differences and increase goodwill mechanically.","caveat":"A DTL may reverse over time; treating every DTL as debt-like at full face value can overstate economic liability.","trap_q":"Why can an asset write-up create a DTL in M&A?","trap_a":"Book basis rises to fair value while tax basis may not; future book expense can exceed tax deductions, creating a taxable temporary difference.","es":"Un write-up contable sin step-up fiscal puede crear un DTL porque la base contable y fiscal divergen.","related":["PPA","Asset write-up","Goodwill"]},{"id":"ACC-SBC","t":"Stock-based compensation","cat":"Accounting","sub":"Non-cash charges","f":4,"d":"SBC is compensation paid with equity-linked awards and is generally recognised as an expense while being non-cash in the period of recognition.","formula":"Net income ↓; add back SBC in CFO under indirect method; equity/APIC often ↑","why":"It matters for dilution, earnings quality and cash-flow interpretation in equity-heavy compensation models.","caveat":"Calling SBC “free” because it is non-cash ignores dilution and the economic cost transferred to shareholders.","trap_q":"If SBC rises by $10, is FCF necessarily $10 higher because it is added back?","trap_a":"No. The accounting add-back raises CFO versus net income, but the economic cost appears through dilution or cash used for buybacks to offset dilution.","es":"SBC es no-cash en el periodo, pero sí tiene costo económico porque diluye a los accionistas.","related":["Dilution","Treasury stock method","Free cash flow"]},{"id":"ACC-DEBT","t":"Debt issuance","cat":"Accounting","sub":"Financing","f":4,"d":"Debt issuance raises cash and creates a liability; interest expense is recognised over time.","formula":"At issuance: Cash ↑; Debt ↑","why":"Debt changes capital structure, liquidity, interest burden and enterprise-to-equity value bridges.","caveat":"Gross debt alone does not show leverage risk; maturity, currency, rate, security and cash generation matter.","trap_q":"Company raises $100 debt and holds the cash. What happens to EV immediately, all else equal?","trap_a":"Equity value is unchanged mechanically; debt rises $100 and cash rises $100, so net debt and EV are unchanged, ignoring fees and market reactions.","es":"Emitir deuda aumenta caja y deuda; si el efectivo queda en balance, net debt no cambia inicialmente.","related":["Net debt","Interest expense","Capital structure"]},{"id":"ACC-BUYBACK","t":"Share repurchase","cat":"Accounting","sub":"Equity","f":4,"d":"A buyback uses cash to repurchase outstanding shares, usually reducing common equity through treasury stock or share cancellation.","formula":"Cash ↓; shares outstanding ↓; equity ↓","why":"Buybacks affect EPS, ownership concentration and capital allocation without changing operating performance.","caveat":"EPS accretion from fewer shares does not necessarily mean value creation; price paid and alternative uses of capital matter.","trap_q":"Can a buyback raise EPS while destroying value?","trap_a":"Yes. If shares are repurchased above intrinsic value or funded with excessive leverage, EPS can rise while economic value per share falls.","es":"Un buyback reduce caja y acciones; puede subir EPS mecánicamente sin garantizar creación de valor.","related":["EPS","Treasury stock","Capital allocation"]},{"id":"ACC-EQUITY","t":"Equity issuance","cat":"Accounting","sub":"Equity","f":4,"d":"An equity issuance raises cash by issuing new shares, increasing common equity and share count.","formula":"Cash ↑; equity ↑; shares outstanding ↑","why":"Equity can fund growth or acquisitions without mandatory interest, but dilutes existing ownership.","caveat":"Dilution in ownership is not automatically value destructive if capital is raised at attractive terms and invested above the cost of capital.","trap_q":"A company issues $100 of equity and keeps the cash. What happens to EV immediately?","trap_a":"Market cap rises by roughly the capital raised if issued at market value, cash rises by the same amount, so EV is broadly unchanged mechanically.","es":"Emitir equity aumenta caja y market cap; al restar la caja en EV, el EV puede quedar prácticamente igual al inicio.","related":["Market cap","Enterprise value","Dilution"]},{"id":"ACC-LEASE","t":"Leases","cat":"Accounting","sub":"Leases","f":3,"d":"Modern lease accounting generally recognises a right-of-use asset and lease liability for most leases, with presentation differences between IFRS and US GAAP.","formula":"ROU asset and lease liability recognised at present value of lease payments","why":"Leases affect leverage, EBITDA comparability, EV adjustments and cash-flow classification.","caveat":"Treatment differs by accounting standard and analyst convention; blindly adding lease liabilities while using unadjusted EBITDA can create inconsistency.","trap_q":"Should lease liabilities always be added to EV?","trap_a":"Only if the numerator and denominator are treated consistently. Many analysts capitalise leases, but the correct convention depends on sector and metric definition.","es":"Los leases pueden parecer deuda económica; lo importante es mantener consistencia entre EV, EBITDA y la contabilidad utilizada.","related":["Enterprise value","EBITDA","Net debt"]},{"id":"ACC-NCI","t":"Non-controlling interest","cat":"Accounting","sub":"Consolidation","f":4,"d":"NCI is the portion of a consolidated subsidiary’s equity not owned by the parent.","formula":"Consolidated statements include 100% of subsidiary operations; NCI represents outside ownership","why":"In EV multiples, NCI is often added to EV when EBITDA includes 100% of a subsidiary, preserving numerator/denominator consistency.","caveat":"NCI accounting values may differ from market value and ownership structures can be complex.","trap_q":"Why add NCI to EV?","trap_a":"Because consolidated EBITDA includes earnings attributable to minority owners; adding NCI aligns the value numerator with the full operating denominator.","es":"Se añade NCI a EV cuando el EBITDA consolidado incluye el 100% de la subsidiaria, aunque la matriz no posea el 100%.","related":["Enterprise value","Consolidation","EV/EBITDA"]},{"id":"ACC-EQUITYM","t":"Equity method investments","cat":"Accounting","sub":"Consolidation","f":3,"d":"Under the equity method, an investor records its share of an investee’s earnings rather than consolidating the investee’s revenue and EBITDA line by line.","formula":"Investment balance changes with share of earnings, dividends and other adjustments","why":"They matter in EV bridges because non-consolidated investments may be deducted from EV while their earnings are excluded from consolidated EBITDA.","caveat":"The right adjustment depends on how the investee contribution appears in the metric being valued.","trap_q":"Why might associates be subtracted from EV?","trap_a":"If the stake is a non-operating asset and its EBITDA is not consolidated, subtracting its value avoids valuing it again through the core business multiple.","es":"Las inversiones por equity method no consolidan EBITDA; por eso su valor puede restarse de EV para mantener consistencia.","related":["Enterprise value","Associates","SOTP"]},{"id":"ACC-CAPEXP","t":"Capitalising vs expensing","cat":"Accounting","sub":"Accounting policy","f":5,"d":"Expensing recognises cost immediately in the income statement; capitalising records an asset and recognises expense over future periods.","formula":"Expense now vs asset now + future D&A","why":"The choice changes current earnings, EBITDA, asset balances and timing of cash-flow presentation even if total cash paid is identical.","caveat":"Accounting rules constrain the choice; aggressive capitalisation can inflate near-term earnings and EBITDA.","trap_q":"Two identical firms, one capitalises a cost and one expenses it. Which has higher first-year EBITDA?","trap_a":"The capitalising firm typically has higher EBITDA because the cost is not in operating expense immediately; later depreciation/amortisation may reduce EBIT.","es":"Capitalizar difiere el gasto contable: sube activos y EBITDA hoy, pero genera D&A futura.","related":["EBITDA","CapEx","Earnings quality"]},{"id":"ACC-CFO","t":"Cash flow classification","cat":"Accounting","sub":"Cash flow statement","f":3,"d":"Cash flows are classified as operating, investing or financing according to their economic nature and accounting rules.","formula":"Ending cash = Beginning cash + CFO + CFI + CFF","why":"Classification helps separate recurring operations from investment and financing decisions.","caveat":"IFRS and US GAAP permit or require different classifications for some interest and dividend cash flows.","trap_q":"Can the same total cash change occur with very different CFO?","trap_a":"Yes. Classification and business activity can shift cash between CFO, CFI and CFF even if ending cash is the same.","es":"El cambio total de caja se divide entre operación, inversión y financiación; la clasificación importa para interpretar la calidad del flujo.","related":["CFO","CFI","CFF"]},{"id":"ACC-RE","t":"Retained earnings","cat":"Accounting","sub":"Equity","f":3,"d":"Retained earnings are cumulative accounting profits retained in the business after dividends and certain adjustments.","formula":"Ending RE = Beginning RE + Net income − Dividends ± adjustments","why":"They connect the income statement to the equity section of the balance sheet.","caveat":"Retained earnings are not a cash account; profits may have been reinvested in inventory, PP&E or other assets.","trap_q":"A company has $1bn retained earnings. Does it have $1bn available cash?","trap_a":"No. Retained earnings are an accounting equity balance, not a pool of cash.","es":"Retained earnings acumula utilidades históricas no distribuidas, pero no significa que esa cantidad exista en efectivo.","related":["Net income","Dividends","Equity"]},{"id":"VAL-EV","t":"Enterprise value","cat":"Valuation","sub":"EV bridge","f":5,"d":"Enterprise value is the value of core operations attributable to all capital providers, commonly approximated as equity value plus debt and other senior claims minus excess cash and non-operating assets.","formula":"EV ≈ Equity Value + Debt + Preferred + NCI − Cash − non-operating investments","why":"EV pairs with pre-interest operating metrics such as Revenue, EBITDA and EBIT.","caveat":"EV bridges are convention-dependent; pensions, leases, associates and restricted cash require judgment.","trap_q":"Can EV be negative?","trap_a":"Yes. A cash-rich company can have cash and investments exceeding market cap plus debt, producing negative calculated EV.","es":"EV representa el valor de las operaciones para todos los proveedores de capital; por eso se suma deuda y se resta caja excedente.","related":["Equity value","Net debt","EV/EBITDA"]},{"id":"VAL-EQV","t":"Equity value","cat":"Valuation","sub":"EV bridge","f":5,"d":"Equity value is the value attributable to common shareholders, typically market price times fully diluted shares outstanding.","formula":"Equity Value = Share Price × Fully Diluted Shares","why":"It is the correct numerator for metrics after interest expense, such as P/E and P/B.","caveat":"Basic market cap may understate diluted value if in-the-money options, RSUs or convertibles add shares.","trap_q":"Can common equity value be negative?","trap_a":"Listed common equity market value cannot fall below zero, although book equity can be negative and option-like equity can approach zero.","es":"Equity value es lo que pertenece al accionista común y se calcula con acciones diluidas, no solo basic shares.","related":["Market cap","Dilution","P/E"]},{"id":"VAL-BRIDGE","t":"EV to Equity bridge","cat":"Valuation","sub":"EV bridge","f":5,"d":"The EV-to-equity bridge removes claims senior to common equity and adds back non-operating assets to move from operating value to shareholder value.","formula":"Equity Value ≈ EV − Debt − Preferred − NCI + Cash + non-operating assets","why":"It is essential in DCF, precedent transactions and trading comps because valuation methods often first estimate EV.","caveat":"Debt-like and cash-like definitions are negotiated in deals; not every liability is debt-like and not every cash balance is freely available.","trap_q":"Why subtract debt from EV to reach equity value?","trap_a":"Debt holders have a prior claim on enterprise value; after satisfying that claim, the residual belongs to equity holders.","es":"Para pasar de EV a equity se restan obligaciones senior y se suman activos no operativos/caja que pertenecen al accionista.","related":["Enterprise value","Net debt","Purchase price"]},{"id":"VAL-FDSO","t":"Fully diluted shares","cat":"Valuation","sub":"Dilution","f":4,"d":"Fully diluted shares include common shares plus incremental shares from dilutive options, RSUs, convertibles and other equity-linked instruments under the relevant convention.","formula":"FDSO = Basic shares + incremental dilutive shares","why":"Using diluted shares avoids overstating per-share value when additional claims can convert into common equity.","caveat":"Methodology varies by instrument, strike price and whether securities are in the money.","trap_q":"Should out-of-the-money options always be included in FDSO?","trap_a":"Usually not under treasury-stock logic because exercising them at current price is not economically dilutive, though deal analyses may use broader definitions.","es":"Las acciones diluidas capturan instrumentos que pueden convertirse en equity y reducir el valor por acción.","related":["Treasury stock method","Options","RSUs"]},{"id":"VAL-TSM","t":"Treasury stock method","cat":"Valuation","sub":"Dilution","f":4,"d":"TSM estimates net new shares from in-the-money options by assuming exercise proceeds are used to repurchase shares at the current market price.","formula":"Incremental shares = Options × (Share Price − Strike) / Share Price","why":"It approximates economic dilution from options without counting the full option amount as new shares.","caveat":"It is sensitive to share price and does not directly model all award types or tax effects.","trap_q":"Why does an option with strike equal to share price create near-zero TSM dilution?","trap_a":"Exercise proceeds would theoretically repurchase roughly the same number of shares issued, leaving little net share increase.","es":"TSM calcula solo las acciones incrementales: asume que el efectivo del ejercicio recompra acciones al precio de mercado.","related":["Options","FDSO","Dilution"]},{"id":"VAL-ND","t":"Net debt","cat":"Valuation","sub":"EV bridge","f":5,"d":"Net debt is gross interest-bearing debt minus cash and cash-like balances, with analyst-specific adjustments.","formula":"Net Debt = Gross Debt − Cash & Cash Equivalents","why":"It captures the net financial claim that bridges EV and equity value and is central to leverage analysis.","caveat":"Restricted cash, leases, pensions and short-term investments require judgment; negative net debt means net cash.","trap_q":"A company raises debt and keeps all proceeds as cash. Does net debt rise immediately?","trap_a":"No. Gross debt and cash rise by the same amount, so net debt is unchanged before fees or other uses.","es":"Net debt resta caja a la deuda bruta; sirve para ver la obligación financiera neta.","related":["Enterprise value","Leverage","Cash"]},{"id":"VAL-MCAP","t":"Market capitalisation","cat":"Valuation","sub":"Equity value","f":4,"d":"Market cap is the market value of a company’s basic common equity: current share price times basic shares outstanding.","formula":"Market Cap = Price × Basic Shares","why":"It is a quick market-based equity value, but valuation work often needs fully diluted equity value instead.","caveat":"It ignores dilution and says nothing directly about debt or enterprise value.","trap_q":"Two companies have identical market cap. Are their operating businesses equally valued?","trap_a":"Not necessarily. Different net debt, preferred stock, NCI or excess cash can make their EVs very different.","es":"Market cap mide el valor bursátil del equity básico; no incorpora deuda ni ajustes de capital structure.","related":["Equity value","Enterprise value","FDSO"]},{"id":"VAL-METHODS","t":"Valuation methodologies","cat":"Valuation","sub":"Method selection","f":5,"d":"Common methods include DCF, trading comparables, precedent transactions, LBO, SOTP, NAV and sector-specific approaches.","formula":"Triangulate rather than rely on one method","why":"Different methods answer different questions: intrinsic value, market-relative value, control value or break-up value.","caveat":"Every method embeds assumptions and market conditions; convergence does not prove correctness.","trap_q":"Which method is always the most accurate?","trap_a":"None. The appropriate method depends on business model, data quality, cyclicality, capital structure and purpose of the analysis.","es":"No existe un método universalmente superior; lo correcto es elegir y triangular según negocio, datos y contexto.","related":["DCF","Trading comps","Precedents"]},{"id":"VAL-DCF","t":"Discounted cash flow","cat":"Valuation","sub":"DCF","f":5,"d":"A DCF estimates intrinsic enterprise or equity value by forecasting future free cash flow and discounting it at a rate consistent with the cash flow’s risk and capital claim.","formula":"PV = Σ FCF_t/(1+r)^t + TV/(1+r)^n","why":"DCF forces explicit assumptions on growth, margins, reinvestment and risk and is independent of current peer multiples.","caveat":"It is highly sensitive to long-dated assumptions, especially WACC and terminal value, and can create false precision.","trap_q":"When would a DCF be less useful?","trap_a":"When cash flows are highly unpredictable, negative for a long period, financial-company capital structures make FCFF awkward, or terminal value dominates with weak visibility.","es":"DCF trae flujos futuros a valor presente; su fortaleza es la lógica económica, pero es muy sensible a supuestos.","related":["WACC","UFCF","Terminal value"]},{"id":"VAL-UFCF","t":"Unlevered free cash flow","cat":"Valuation","sub":"DCF","f":5,"d":"UFCF is cash flow available to all capital providers before interest payments, typically derived from after-tax operating profit plus non-cash charges minus CapEx and increases in NWC.","formula":"UFCF = EBIT×(1−T) + D&A − CapEx − ΔNWC","why":"It pairs with WACC and yields enterprise value because it is pre-financing cash flow.","caveat":"Tax calculations and non-recurring items require normalisation; using EBITDA without reinvestment can overstate cash generation.","trap_q":"Why not subtract interest in UFCF?","trap_a":"Because WACC already reflects the cost of debt and equity; subtracting interest would mix financing into a pre-financing cash flow and double count leverage effects.","es":"UFCF es flujo antes de financiación, por eso se descuenta con WACC y produce EV.","related":["WACC","FCFE","NOPAT"]},{"id":"VAL-FCFE","t":"Free cash flow to equity","cat":"Valuation","sub":"DCF","f":4,"d":"FCFE is cash flow available to common equity after operating needs, capex, working capital, interest and net debt financing.","formula":"FCFE ≈ Net Income + D&A − CapEx − ΔNWC + Net Borrowing","why":"Discounting FCFE at cost of equity directly estimates equity value.","caveat":"Debt policy must be forecast consistently, making FCFE harder when leverage changes materially.","trap_q":"When might FCFE be preferable to FCFF?","trap_a":"For businesses where debt financing is integral and predictable, or when directly valuing equity with a stable leverage policy.","es":"FCFE es caja residual para accionistas después de financiación; se descuenta al cost of equity.","related":["Cost of equity","FCFF","Net borrowing"]},{"id":"VAL-WACC","t":"WACC","cat":"Valuation","sub":"DCF","f":5,"d":"WACC is the blended required return of debt and equity capital, weighted by market values and adjusted for the tax deductibility of interest where appropriate.","formula":"WACC = E/(D+E)×Ke + D/(D+E)×Kd×(1−T)","why":"It is the discount rate for unlevered free cash flow because UFCF belongs to both debt and equity providers.","caveat":"Weights should be market-based and target capital structure may differ from current structure; country and size risk adjustments require care.","trap_q":"Can WACC be lower than after-tax cost of debt?","trap_a":"Under standard positive weights and a cost of equity above after-tax debt cost, no; unusual negative rates or unconventional capital structures require careful definition.","es":"WACC mezcla el retorno exigido por deuda y equity usando pesos de mercado; se usa con UFCF.","related":["CAPM","Cost of debt","Capital structure"]},{"id":"VAL-CAPM","t":"CAPM","cat":"Valuation","sub":"DCF","f":5,"d":"CAPM estimates cost of equity as the risk-free rate plus beta times the equity risk premium.","formula":"Ke = Rf + β×ERP","why":"It links required equity return to systematic market risk and is widely used in WACC.","caveat":"Beta and ERP estimates are noisy; CAPM omits some risks and may need country-risk or other adjustments.","trap_q":"If beta doubles, does the company become twice as risky in every sense?","trap_a":"No. Beta measures sensitivity to systematic market returns, not total risk, operational risk or default risk.","es":"CAPM estima cost of equity con riesgo sistemático: risk-free más beta por equity risk premium.","related":["Beta","ERP","Cost of equity"]},{"id":"VAL-BETA","t":"Beta","cat":"Valuation","sub":"DCF","f":5,"d":"Beta measures the sensitivity of an asset’s returns to market returns; in valuation, peer betas are often unlevered and relevered to a target capital structure.","formula":"βu ≈ βl / [1+(1−T)D/E]; βl,target ≈ βu×[1+(1−T)D/E]","why":"Unlevering separates business risk from financing leverage; relevering applies the target capital structure.","caveat":"Historical beta is unstable and depends on lookback, frequency, index and capital structure.","trap_q":"Why can two identical businesses have different levered betas?","trap_a":"Different financial leverage amplifies equity sensitivity; the more levered equity is a thinner residual claim on the same enterprise.","es":"Beta apalancada incluye riesgo financiero; unlever/relever busca aislar el riesgo operativo y luego aplicar la deuda objetivo.","related":["CAPM","Leverage","Peer group"]},{"id":"VAL-RFR","t":"Risk-free rate","cat":"Valuation","sub":"DCF","f":4,"d":"The risk-free rate is the return on a default-free investment in the valuation currency over a relevant maturity, commonly proxied by high-quality sovereign yields.","formula":"Nominal cash flows should use a nominal risk-free rate in the same currency","why":"It anchors CAPM and discount rates and embeds prevailing inflation and term expectations.","caveat":"No sovereign is literally riskless; maturity and currency mismatches can distort valuation.","trap_q":"Should a USD DCF use a Bolivian local-currency sovereign yield as the risk-free rate?","trap_a":"Not directly. The discount rate should be internally consistent with the cash-flow currency; country risk, if needed, is handled separately.","es":"El risk-free debe ser consistente con la moneda y nominalidad de los flujos; no se elige solo por domicilio de la empresa.","related":["CAPM","Country risk","Currency"]},{"id":"VAL-ERP","t":"Equity risk premium","cat":"Valuation","sub":"DCF","f":4,"d":"ERP is the additional return investors require for bearing broad equity-market risk over the risk-free rate.","formula":"Market expected return = Rf + ERP","why":"It is a major input to cost of equity and changes materially affect DCF values.","caveat":"ERP is not directly observable; historical, implied and survey estimates can differ.","trap_q":"If risk-free rates fall, must ERP also fall?","trap_a":"No. ERP can move independently and may rise in stressed markets even as risk-free yields fall.","es":"ERP es la prima exigida por riesgo de mercado sobre el activo libre de riesgo; no es observable directamente.","related":["CAPM","Risk-free rate","Cost of equity"]},{"id":"VAL-COD","t":"Cost of debt","cat":"Valuation","sub":"DCF","f":4,"d":"Cost of debt is the market-required yield on a company’s borrowing, reflecting base rates, credit spread and instrument features.","formula":"Pre-tax Kd ≈ reference rate + credit spread","why":"It feeds WACC and reflects default, liquidity and duration risk more accurately than historical coupon rates.","caveat":"Book interest expense divided by debt is backward-looking and may not equal marginal market borrowing cost.","trap_q":"Should WACC use the average coupon on existing debt?","trap_a":"Usually no. WACC should reflect the current market cost of debt for comparable risk and maturity, not merely historical coupons.","es":"Cost of debt debe reflejar el costo marginal de mercado, no solo el cupón histórico.","related":["WACC","Credit spread","Yield"]},{"id":"VAL-TV","t":"Terminal value","cat":"Valuation","sub":"DCF","f":5,"d":"Terminal value captures the value of cash flows beyond the explicit forecast period, usually through Gordon growth or an exit multiple.","formula":"Gordon TV = FCF_(n+1)/(WACC−g); Exit TV = Metric_n × Exit Multiple","why":"It prevents the forecast from stopping economically at year five or ten and often represents a large share of DCF value.","caveat":"Small changes in WACC, growth or exit multiple can dominate valuation, so terminal assumptions require strong economic discipline.","trap_q":"Can terminal growth exceed WACC?","trap_a":"Not in a stable Gordon-growth model; the formula would become unstable and imply an economically nonsensical infinite or negative denominator.","es":"Terminal value captura el valor después del forecast explícito y suele ser la parte más sensible del DCF.","related":["Gordon growth","Exit multiple","WACC"]},{"id":"VAL-GORDON","t":"Gordon growth method","cat":"Valuation","sub":"DCF","f":5,"d":"The Gordon method values a perpetually growing stream of free cash flow using a stable long-term growth rate below the discount rate.","formula":"TV = FCF_(n+1)/(WACC−g)","why":"It ties terminal value to cash-flow economics rather than a market multiple.","caveat":"The business must be in a mature steady state; implausible growth, margins or reinvestment make the result unreliable.","trap_q":"Can a company grow forever faster than nominal GDP in the valuation currency?","trap_a":"Not sustainably at scale. A perpetual growth rate should be consistent with long-run economic and inflation constraints.","es":"Gordon Growth supone un estado estable perpetuo; g debe ser razonable y menor que WACC.","related":["Terminal value","Long-term growth","Reinvestment"]},{"id":"VAL-EXIT","t":"Exit multiple method","cat":"Valuation","sub":"DCF","f":4,"d":"The exit-multiple method applies a market multiple to a terminal-period financial metric to estimate terminal enterprise or equity value.","formula":"TV = Terminal metric × Exit multiple","why":"It anchors terminal value to observable market valuation conventions and is intuitive in banker models.","caveat":"It makes an intrinsic DCF partly market-relative and can import cyclically high or low multiples into the terminal year.","trap_q":"Should exit multiple automatically equal today’s trading multiple?","trap_a":"No. It should reflect expected terminal growth, margins, returns and market conditions; current multiple is only a reference point.","es":"El exit multiple aplica un múltiplo al año terminal; es práctico pero introduce supuestos de mercado en el DCF.","related":["Trading comps","Terminal value","EV/EBITDA"]},{"id":"VAL-MID","t":"Mid-year convention","cat":"Valuation","sub":"DCF","f":3,"d":"Mid-year convention assumes cash flows are generated throughout the year rather than entirely at year-end, so each annual flow is discounted for roughly half a period less.","formula":"Discount exponent ≈ t−0.5","why":"It better reflects intra-year cash generation and usually increases present value versus year-end discounting.","caveat":"It is still an approximation and may be inappropriate for highly seasonal or lumpy businesses.","trap_q":"Does mid-year convention change operating forecasts?","trap_a":"No. It changes discount timing, not the forecasted cash-flow amounts themselves.","es":"Mid-year convention reconoce que la caja se genera durante el año, no toda el 31 de diciembre.","related":["Discount factor","Stub period","DCF"]},{"id":"VAL-STUB","t":"Stub period","cat":"Valuation","sub":"DCF","f":3,"d":"A stub period is a partial first forecast period between the valuation date and the next fiscal period-end.","formula":"Discount using the fraction of a year from valuation date","why":"Stub treatment keeps timing and discounting consistent when valuation occurs mid-year.","caveat":"Using full-year cash flow without prorating or adjusting timing can overstate value.","trap_q":"Valuation date is 30 September and fiscal year ends 31 December. Use a full year-one discount?","trap_a":"No. The first period is only about three months; forecast and discount timing should reflect the stub.","es":"El stub ajusta el primer periodo cuando la fecha de valoración no coincide con el cierre fiscal.","related":["Mid-year convention","Discount factor","Forecast period"]},{"id":"VAL-SENS","t":"Sensitivity analysis","cat":"Valuation","sub":"DCF","f":5,"d":"Sensitivity analysis shows how valuation changes when key assumptions such as WACC, terminal growth or exit multiple vary across plausible ranges.","formula":"Recalculate value across a matrix of key assumptions","why":"It reveals model fragility and communicates a valuation range rather than false point-estimate precision.","caveat":"A sensitivity table is only as credible as the ranges chosen and does not replace scenario analysis on operating drivers.","trap_q":"If every sensitivity produces the same value, is the DCF robust?","trap_a":"Possibly, but first check the model. Important assumptions normally change value; flat outputs can indicate a broken formula or an insensitive driver.","es":"Sensitivity analysis muestra cuánto depende la valoración de supuestos clave; sirve para ver riesgo de modelo.","related":["Scenario analysis","WACC","Terminal value"]},{"id":"VAL-COMPS","t":"Trading comparables","cat":"Valuation","sub":"Relative valuation","f":5,"d":"Trading comps value a company relative to publicly traded peers using market-based multiples on consistent financial metrics.","formula":"Implied EV = Selected multiple × Target metric","why":"They reflect current market pricing and are useful when a credible peer set exists.","caveat":"No peer is identical; accounting, growth, margin, geography and capital structure differences require judgment.","trap_q":"Company A trades at 8x and B at 12x. Is A definitely cheaper?","trap_a":"No. B may deserve a higher multiple because of faster growth, better margins, higher ROIC, lower risk or superior cash conversion.","es":"Comps compara múltiplos de pares, pero un múltiplo menor no significa automáticamente que la empresa esté barata.","related":["EV/EBITDA","P/E","Peer selection"]},{"id":"VAL-PREC","t":"Precedent transactions","cat":"Valuation","sub":"Relative valuation","f":5,"d":"Precedents use valuation multiples paid in comparable M&A transactions to infer a control value for the target.","formula":"Implied value = Selected precedent multiple × Target metric","why":"They incorporate real transaction pricing, control premiums and deal-market conditions.","caveat":"Deals may be stale, strategic rationales differ, synergies may influence price and disclosure can be limited.","trap_q":"Why do precedents often show higher values than trading comps?","trap_a":"They can include control premiums and part of expected synergies, but this is a tendency rather than a rule.","es":"Precedents usa precios reales de M&A y suele incorporar control premium; comparabilidad y fecha del deal importan mucho.","related":["Control premium","Trading comps","Synergies"]},{"id":"VAL-SOTP","t":"Sum-of-the-parts","cat":"Valuation","sub":"Method selection","f":4,"d":"SOTP values distinct business segments separately using the method and peer set appropriate to each, then adds them and adjusts for corporate items and net debt.","formula":"Equity Value = Σ Segment EVs − Net Debt ± corporate/non-operating adjustments","why":"It is useful for conglomerates or companies whose divisions have materially different economics.","caveat":"Segment disclosure, central costs, tax leakage and intercompany dependencies can complicate clean separation.","trap_q":"Can SOTP always unlock a “conglomerate discount”?","trap_a":"No. A discount may reflect real central costs, tax leakage, weaker governance or inability to separate assets cleanly.","es":"SOTP valora cada segmento por separado; es útil cuando un solo múltiplo consolidado oculta negocios muy distintos.","related":["Conglomerate discount","NAV","Segment valuation"]},{"id":"VAL-NAV","t":"Net asset value","cat":"Valuation","sub":"Asset-based valuation","f":3,"d":"NAV estimates equity value by marking assets and liabilities to current or appraised values rather than relying primarily on earnings multiples.","formula":"NAV = Fair value of assets − Fair value of liabilities","why":"It is common for real estate, natural resources, investment companies and other asset-heavy businesses.","caveat":"Appraisals can be subjective and NAV may ignore franchise value or future growth.","trap_q":"Would NAV usually be the primary method for a software company?","trap_a":"Usually not, because much of a software company’s value comes from future cash flows and intangible franchise economics rather than separable balance-sheet assets.","es":"NAV funciona mejor cuando el valor está en activos identificables; es menos útil para negocios asset-light con mucho valor intangible.","related":["Real estate","SOTP","Liquidation value"]},{"id":"VAL-LIQ","t":"Liquidation value","cat":"Valuation","sub":"Asset-based valuation","f":3,"d":"Liquidation value estimates proceeds available to claimholders if assets are sold and liabilities/closure costs are paid.","formula":"Equity liquidation value = Recoverable asset proceeds − liabilities − wind-down costs","why":"It provides a downside framework for distressed or asset-heavy situations.","caveat":"Forced-sale discounts, priority claims, contingent liabilities and wind-down costs can be difficult to estimate.","trap_q":"Can liquidation value exceed going-concern value?","trap_a":"Yes, if assets are worth more sold separately than operating together, though this is uncommon for healthy franchise businesses.","es":"Liquidation value estima cuánto quedaría al vender activos y pagar obligaciones; es una referencia de downside.","related":["Distress","Recovery","NAV"]},{"id":"VAL-DDM","t":"Dividend discount model","cat":"Valuation","sub":"Equity valuation","f":3,"d":"DDM values equity as the present value of expected future dividends to shareholders.","formula":"Equity Value = Σ Dividend_t/(1+Ke)^t + terminal dividend value","why":"It can be useful for mature dividend-paying financial institutions where FCFF is less intuitive.","caveat":"Dividend policy can differ from economic capacity to pay; firms with unstable or no dividends are hard to value with DDM.","trap_q":"A bank could pay more dividends but chooses not to. Does DDM necessarily capture full value?","trap_a":"Not unless the model reflects sustainable future payouts; mechanical use of current dividends can understate distributable value.","es":"DDM descuenta dividendos esperados al cost of equity; es común en bancos maduros pero depende de política de payout.","related":["Banks","Cost of equity","Payout ratio"]},{"id":"MA-RATIONALE","t":"Strategic rationale","cat":"M&A","sub":"Strategy","f":5,"d":"Strategic rationale is the economic reason a buyer expects an acquisition to create more value than remaining standalone.","formula":"Value creation ≈ standalone values + synergies − premium − integration/transaction costs","why":"A strong rationale links specific capabilities, markets, products or costs to measurable value creation.","caveat":"Vague claims about “scale” or “strategic fit” are insufficient without quantifying benefits, risks and alternatives.","trap_q":"Can a strategically sensible deal still destroy shareholder value?","trap_a":"Yes. Overpaying, poor integration, unrealistic synergies or expensive financing can destroy value despite sound industrial logic.","es":"La lógica estratégica debe traducirse en creación de valor cuantificable; una buena empresa comprada a mal precio puede ser un mal deal.","related":["Synergies","Premium","Integration"]},{"id":"MA-BUYSIDE","t":"Buy-side M&A process","cat":"M&A","sub":"Process","f":4,"d":"A buy-side process identifies targets, evaluates strategic fit and valuation, approaches sellers, conducts diligence, negotiates terms, secures financing/approvals and closes.","formula":"Screen → approach → NDA → diligence → valuation/offer → SPA → approvals → close","why":"It tests a buyer’s ability to allocate capital and manage execution risk.","caveat":"Process varies widely depending on auction dynamics, public/private status and regulatory complexity.","trap_q":"Why might a buyer prefer a bilateral process?","trap_a":"It can reduce competitive tension, preserve confidentiality and allow tailored negotiation, though the seller may demand stronger certainty or price.","es":"Buy-side empieza con target screening y termina en signing/closing; la dinámica cambia si es bilateral o auction.","related":["Target screening","Due diligence","SPA"]},{"id":"MA-SELLSIDE","t":"Sell-side M&A process","cat":"M&A","sub":"Process","f":4,"d":"A sell-side process prepares the asset, markets it to buyers, manages competitive tension, diligence and bids, then negotiates and executes a transaction.","formula":"Preparation → teaser/NDA → CIM → bids → management/diligence → final bids → exclusivity/SPA → close","why":"A structured process can maximise price and certainty while controlling information release.","caveat":"More bidders can increase price but also execution burden, leaks and management distraction.","trap_q":"Why run an auction instead of approaching one buyer?","trap_a":"Competition can improve price and terms, benchmark demand and reduce dependence on one counterparty.","es":"Sell-side organiza información, compradores y rondas de oferta para maximizar valor y certeza de ejecución.","related":["Auction","CIM","Final bids"]},{"id":"MA-TEASER","t":"Teaser","cat":"M&A","sub":"Process documents","f":3,"d":"A teaser is a short marketing document describing an asset anonymously or with limited identification to generate buyer interest.","formula":"No formula","why":"It screens interest before sharing sensitive information and obtaining NDAs.","caveat":"Too much detail can reveal identity; too little can fail to attract credible buyers.","trap_q":"Does a teaser normally contain the full valuation model?","trap_a":"No. It is an initial marketing document, not the data room or full financial package.","es":"El teaser es un documento breve para despertar interés antes del NDA y del CIM.","related":["NDA","CIM","Sell-side"]},{"id":"MA-NDA","t":"Non-disclosure agreement","cat":"M&A","sub":"Process documents","f":3,"d":"An NDA contractually restricts how confidential deal information may be used and disclosed.","formula":"No formula","why":"It protects sensitive information before a potential buyer receives detailed materials.","caveat":"Terms can include standstills, non-solicit provisions and permitted-recipient rules; enforceability varies.","trap_q":"Why might a seller require an NDA before naming the company?","trap_a":"To limit misuse or leakage of competitively sensitive information once identity and details are disclosed.","es":"El NDA protege la información confidencial antes de abrir materiales más detallados.","related":["Teaser","Data room","Confidentiality"]},{"id":"MA-CIM","t":"CIM / Information Memorandum","cat":"M&A","sub":"Process documents","f":4,"d":"A CIM is a detailed sell-side marketing document covering the business, industry, financials, strategy and investment highlights.","formula":"No formula","why":"It gives buyers enough structured information to evaluate the opportunity and submit informed bids.","caveat":"It is prepared by the seller and advisers, so buyers must validate claims through diligence.","trap_q":"Is the CIM a substitute for due diligence?","trap_a":"No. It is a marketing and information document; buyers independently verify assumptions and risks.","es":"El CIM presenta el negocio en detalle, pero no reemplaza la due diligence.","related":["Teaser","Data room","Management presentation"]},{"id":"MA-DATAROOM","t":"Virtual data room","cat":"M&A","sub":"Due diligence","f":3,"d":"A data room is a controlled repository containing financial, legal, tax, commercial and operational information for diligence.","formula":"No formula","why":"It centralises disclosure, access controls and audit trails during a transaction.","caveat":"Incomplete or inconsistent uploads can create diligence risk and negotiation friction.","trap_q":"Why not send all files by email?","trap_a":"A VDR provides permissions, version control, tracking and structured access to sensitive data.","es":"El data room centraliza documentación sensible y permite controlar quién ve qué durante la diligencia.","related":["Due diligence","NDA","Q&A"]},{"id":"MA-DD","t":"Due diligence","cat":"M&A","sub":"Due diligence","f":5,"d":"Due diligence is the buyer’s structured investigation of financial, commercial, legal, tax, operational, IT and other risks before committing to a transaction.","formula":"No single formula","why":"It tests valuation assumptions, identifies liabilities and informs price, protections, financing and integration planning.","caveat":"Diligence reduces uncertainty but cannot eliminate it; scope, time and data access constrain findings.","trap_q":"If diligence finds a problem, must the buyer walk away?","trap_a":"No. The buyer can reprice, change structure, demand indemnities/escrow, adjust conditions or accept the risk.","es":"Due diligence valida supuestos y riesgos; un hallazgo puede cambiar precio, estructura o protecciones, no necesariamente matar el deal.","related":["Quality of earnings","SPA","Representations"]},{"id":"MA-IOI","t":"Indication of interest","cat":"M&A","sub":"Process","f":3,"d":"An IOI is a preliminary, usually non-binding expression of valuation range and transaction interest.","formula":"No formula","why":"It helps sellers narrow the bidder field before granting deeper access.","caveat":"Terms are preliminary and often subject to diligence, financing and approvals.","trap_q":"Is an IOI the same as a signed purchase agreement?","trap_a":"No. It is typically non-binding and far earlier in the process.","es":"El IOI es una oferta preliminar indicativa, normalmente no vinculante.","related":["LOI","Auction","Bid"]},{"id":"MA-LOI","t":"Letter of intent","cat":"M&A","sub":"Process","f":4,"d":"An LOI outlines key proposed deal terms and can include binding provisions such as exclusivity or confidentiality even if purchase terms remain non-binding.","formula":"No formula","why":"It aligns parties on major economics before spending more time and cost on definitive documents.","caveat":"Legal effect varies by clause and jurisdiction; assuming the whole LOI is non-binding is unsafe.","trap_q":"Can an LOI contain binding terms?","trap_a":"Yes. Exclusivity, confidentiality, costs and governing-law provisions can be binding even when price/closing remain non-binding.","es":"El LOI alinea términos principales; algunas cláusulas pueden ser vinculantes aunque el deal aún no lo sea.","related":["Exclusivity","SPA","Binding offer"]},{"id":"MA-SPA","t":"Share purchase agreement","cat":"M&A","sub":"Definitive documents","f":5,"d":"An SPA is the definitive contract governing a share acquisition, including price mechanics, representations, warranties, covenants, conditions and remedies.","formula":"Purchase price mechanics defined contractually","why":"It allocates economic and legal risk between buyer and seller and converts commercial agreement into enforceable terms.","caveat":"Small drafting differences can shift significant value through working-capital adjustments, indemnities or closing conditions.","trap_q":"Why does a banker care about the SPA if lawyers draft it?","trap_a":"Because purchase price mechanics, financing conditions and closing adjustments directly affect deal economics and certainty.","es":"El SPA define legalmente precio, ajustes, garantías, condiciones y remedios; impacta la economía real del deal.","related":["Locked box","Completion accounts","Representations"]},{"id":"MA-SIGNCLOSE","t":"Signing vs closing","cat":"M&A","sub":"Execution","f":4,"d":"Signing is execution of definitive agreements; closing is the later transfer of ownership and consideration once required conditions are satisfied.","formula":"No formula","why":"The gap matters for regulatory approvals, financing certainty, interim covenants and deal risk.","caveat":"Some deals sign and close simultaneously, while regulated transactions may have long gaps.","trap_q":"Can a signed deal still fail to close?","trap_a":"Yes. Conditions, regulatory blocks, financing failures or contractual termination rights can prevent closing.","es":"Signing firma el contrato; closing transfiere propiedad y dinero. Entre ambos puede existir riesgo de ejecución.","related":["Conditions precedent","Regulatory approval","MAC"]},{"id":"MA-PP","t":"Purchase price","cat":"M&A","sub":"Deal mechanics","f":5,"d":"Purchase price is the consideration paid for the target equity or assets, subject to the transaction’s agreed debt/cash and working-capital mechanics.","formula":"Equity purchase price often = EV − net debt ± adjustments","why":"It determines seller proceeds and feeds sources & uses, accretion/dilution and returns.","caveat":"Headline transaction value can refer to equity value or enterprise value, so definitions must be explicit.","trap_q":"A press release says “$5bn transaction value.” Is that definitely equity purchase price?","trap_a":"No. It may refer to enterprise value, equity value or another headline measure; check the definition.","es":"Purchase price puede referirse a equity o EV según el contexto; siempre hay que identificar la definición exacta.","related":["Enterprise value","Equity purchase price","Sources & uses"]},{"id":"MA-SU","t":"Sources & uses","cat":"M&A","sub":"Deal mechanics","f":5,"d":"Sources & uses shows what cash is required to complete a transaction and how that requirement is funded.","formula":"Sources = Uses","why":"Uses commonly include equity purchase price, debt refinance and fees; sources include cash, new debt, new equity or rollover.","caveat":"Treatment of minimum cash, existing debt, fees and financing expenses must be consistent.","trap_q":"Why refinance target debt in uses if EV already includes debt?","trap_a":"The EV-to-equity bridge and transaction funding are different views: existing debt may need to be repaid at closing even though it was already reflected in EV.","es":"Sources & Uses asegura que todo uso de fondos tenga una fuente; conecta purchase price con financiación y fees.","related":["Purchase price","Debt refinancing","Financing fees"]},{"id":"MA-CONS","t":"Cash vs stock consideration","cat":"M&A","sub":"Deal mechanics","f":5,"d":"Acquisition consideration can be cash, stock or a mix; each choice changes financing cost, ownership, risk-sharing and accretion/dilution.","formula":"Stock shares issued ≈ Stock consideration / Acquirer share price","why":"Cash preserves ownership but uses liquidity or debt capacity; stock shares valuation risk with the seller and protects cash.","caveat":"The “cheapest” financing on EPS is not necessarily the best economic choice.","trap_q":"When might stock be preferable even if it dilutes EPS?","trap_a":"When preserving balance-sheet capacity matters, acquirer shares are highly valued, uncertainty is high or risk-sharing with the seller is desirable.","es":"Cash y stock cambian liquidez, leverage y reparto de riesgo; la mejor opción no se decide solo por EPS.","related":["Accretion/dilution","Financing mix","Share issuance"]},{"id":"MA-AD","t":"Accretion / dilution","cat":"M&A","sub":"Accretion dilution","f":5,"d":"A deal is EPS-accretive if pro forma EPS exceeds standalone acquirer EPS and dilutive if it is lower.","formula":"Pro forma EPS = Combined adjusted net income / Pro forma shares","why":"It is a common transaction metric and captures financing, synergies, purchase accounting and share issuance.","caveat":"EPS accretion is not equivalent to value creation and can be engineered through leverage, accounting or buying a lower-P/E target.","trap_q":"Can an accretive deal destroy value?","trap_a":"Yes. The buyer can overpay or take excessive risk while still increasing EPS mechanically.","es":"Accretion/dilution mide EPS pro forma, no creación de valor. Un deal puede ser accretive y destruir valor.","related":["P/E","Synergies","Pro forma EPS"]},{"id":"MA-PEACC","t":"P/E accretion intuition","cat":"M&A","sub":"Accretion dilution","f":5,"d":"In a simple all-stock deal with no synergies or purchase-accounting effects, a higher-P/E acquirer buying a lower-P/E target tends to be EPS-accretive.","formula":"Earnings yield = 1/P/E; issuing expensive equity to buy a higher earnings yield can raise EPS","why":"The intuition comes from exchanging a lower earnings yield equity currency for a higher earnings yield target.","caveat":"Premium, synergies, share issuance terms, tax, purchase accounting and different growth can overturn the simple rule.","trap_q":"Acquirer at 20x P/E buys target at 12x entirely with stock. Guaranteed accretive?","trap_a":"No. It is a useful initial intuition, not a guarantee; premium and transaction adjustments can make the deal dilutive.","es":"El atajo de P/E funciona por earnings yield, pero premium, sinergias y accounting pueden cambiar el resultado.","related":["Accretion/dilution","Earnings yield","Stock consideration"]},{"id":"MA-SYN","t":"Synergies","cat":"M&A","sub":"Synergies","f":5,"d":"Synergies are incremental benefits expected from combining businesses, commonly cost savings, revenue uplift, tax benefits or capital efficiencies.","formula":"Value of recurring synergy ≈ after-tax synergy / discount rate, adjusted for timing and implementation costs","why":"Synergies can justify part of a premium and are central to maximum affordable price.","caveat":"Revenue synergies are generally harder to underwrite than cost synergies; execution timing and one-off costs matter.","trap_q":"If annual pre-tax cost synergies are $50 and tax is 25%, is $50 the annual value to shareholders?","trap_a":"No. After-tax recurring benefit is about $37.5 before considering implementation costs, timing and reinvestment.","es":"Las sinergias deben valorarse después de impuestos y netas de costos de implementación; no todo synergy claim vale lo mismo.","related":["Cost synergies","Revenue synergies","Premium"]},{"id":"MA-BESYN","t":"Break-even synergies","cat":"M&A","sub":"Synergies","f":4,"d":"Break-even synergies are the amount of synergy required for the transaction to meet a specified value or EPS threshold.","formula":"Required synergy = shortfall before synergies / after-tax or per-share conversion factor","why":"They show how much execution burden is needed to justify price or avoid dilution.","caveat":"Break-even EPS synergy and break-even value-creation synergy are different concepts.","trap_q":"If a deal needs $200m synergies just to avoid EPS dilution, is it attractive?","trap_a":"Not necessarily. Compare that requirement with credible achievable synergies, integration costs and value creation—not just EPS.","es":"Break-even synergies muestra cuánto beneficio hace falta para alcanzar un umbral; no prueba por sí solo que el deal cree valor.","related":["Accretion/dilution","Synergies","Premium"]},{"id":"MA-PPA","t":"Purchase price allocation","cat":"M&A","sub":"Purchase accounting","f":5,"d":"PPA allocates acquisition consideration to identifiable assets and liabilities at fair value, with the residual generally recorded as goodwill.","formula":"Purchase price → FV net assets + identifiable intangibles + goodwill","why":"It drives post-deal D&A, deferred taxes, goodwill and pro forma earnings.","caveat":"Fair-value estimates and useful lives require judgment and may differ between accounting and tax bases.","trap_q":"Why can PPA make an economically attractive acquisition look EPS-dilutive?","trap_a":"Asset and intangible write-ups can create additional depreciation/amortisation that reduces accounting EPS despite strong cash economics.","es":"PPA reparte el precio entre activos/liabilities a fair value y goodwill; puede crear D&A que reduce EPS.","related":["Goodwill","Asset write-up","DTL"]},{"id":"MA-WRITEUP","t":"Asset write-up","cat":"M&A","sub":"Purchase accounting","f":5,"d":"An asset write-up increases an acquired asset from book value to fair value in purchase accounting.","formula":"Fair-value step-up = FV − book value","why":"It can better reflect acquired economics but creates future depreciation/amortisation and may create deferred taxes.","caveat":"Not every asset is stepped up the same way; tax deductibility depends on transaction structure.","trap_q":"Inventory is written up by $20 in PPA. What can happen when it is sold?","trap_a":"The step-up flows through COGS, reducing post-deal gross profit and earnings for the period in which the inventory turns.","es":"Un write-up aumenta el valor contable del activo y suele generar gasto futuro adicional cuando ese activo se consume.","related":["PPA","Inventory","Deferred tax liability"]},{"id":"MA-GW","t":"M&A goodwill creation","cat":"M&A","sub":"Purchase accounting","f":5,"d":"M&A goodwill is the residual after allocating purchase consideration to fair-valued identifiable net assets and relevant ownership interests.","formula":"Goodwill = consideration + NCI + prior interest − FV net identifiable assets","why":"It absorbs the unidentifiable portion of purchase premium, including expected synergies and assembled workforce.","caveat":"Higher goodwill does not itself prove overpayment; interpretation depends on economics and asset recognition.","trap_q":"If identifiable intangible values increase in PPA, what happens to goodwill all else equal?","trap_a":"Goodwill falls because more of the purchase price is allocated to separately identifiable assets.","es":"En M&A, goodwill es el residuo después de valorar activos netos identificables; más intangibles reconocidos implica menos goodwill.","related":["PPA","Intangibles","Control premium"]},{"id":"MA-PREM","t":"Offer / control premium","cat":"M&A","sub":"Pricing","f":5,"d":"A premium is the amount paid above an unaffected market price or other reference value, often reflecting control and expected synergies.","formula":"Premium % = Offer price / Unaffected price − 1","why":"Premium analysis helps assess seller value capture and compare transaction pricing.","caveat":"The unaffected price may itself reflect rumours, undervaluation or overvaluation; a high premium is not automatically overpayment.","trap_q":"Can a 40% premium be cheaper than a 20% premium?","trap_a":"Yes. If the lower reference price is more undervalued or synergies are larger, absolute economics can still be more attractive.","es":"El premium compara el precio ofertado con una referencia; un premium alto no significa automáticamente que se pagó demasiado.","related":["Control premium","Precedents","Synergies"]},{"id":"MA-MAXPRICE","t":"Maximum purchase price","cat":"M&A","sub":"Pricing","f":5,"d":"Maximum purchase price is the highest price a buyer can pay while meeting its required return, value-creation or accretion threshold.","formula":"Max price ≈ standalone target value + buyer-capturable synergy value − required value creation − costs","why":"It converts strategic benefits and financing constraints into a disciplined bid ceiling.","caveat":"The answer depends on synergy ownership, tax, integration cost, financing, risk and required return.","trap_q":"Should a buyer pay the full present value of expected synergies to the seller?","trap_a":"Usually that would leave little synergy value for the buyer; competitive dynamics determine how synergy value is shared.","es":"El máximo precio depende de cuánto valor standalone y sinergias puede capturar el comprador sin destruir su retorno requerido.","related":["Synergies","Premium","IRR"]},{"id":"MA-LOCK","t":"Locked-box pricing","cat":"M&A","sub":"Purchase price mechanics","f":4,"d":"Locked-box pricing fixes equity price using a historical balance sheet and protects the buyer against value leakage between the locked-box date and closing.","formula":"Fixed price based on agreed locked-box accounts; leakage rules replace closing true-up","why":"It gives price certainty and avoids post-closing completion-account disputes.","caveat":"Buyer relies heavily on historical accounts and leakage protections; long signing-to-close periods increase exposure.","trap_q":"How does locked box differ from completion accounts?","trap_a":"Locked box generally fixes price upfront from a past balance sheet; completion accounts true up debt, cash and working capital around closing.","es":"Locked box fija el precio con una fecha histórica y controla leakage, en vez de hacer un true-up completo al cierre.","related":["Completion accounts","Leakage","SPA"]},{"id":"MA-COMPACC","t":"Completion accounts","cat":"M&A","sub":"Purchase price mechanics","f":4,"d":"Completion accounts adjust transaction price after or at closing using actual debt, cash, working capital and other agreed balances at the completion date.","formula":"Final equity price = agreed EV − closing net debt ± working-capital/other true-ups","why":"They align price with the balance sheet actually delivered at closing.","caveat":"Definitions and accounting policies can create disputes, so the SPA must specify methodology carefully.","trap_q":"Why negotiate a normal working-capital target?","trap_a":"To prevent the seller from extracting value by delivering the business with unusually low working capital at closing.","es":"Completion accounts ajusta el precio usando cifras reales de cierre, especialmente net debt y working capital.","related":["Locked box","Working capital target","Net debt"]},{"id":"MA-EARNOUT","t":"Earn-out","cat":"M&A","sub":"Deal structure","f":3,"d":"An earn-out makes part of consideration contingent on future performance or milestones.","formula":"Total consideration = upfront payment + contingent payment","why":"It can bridge valuation disagreements and share post-closing performance risk.","caveat":"It creates measurement, control and incentive disputes because the buyer influences the acquired business after closing.","trap_q":"Why might a seller dislike an earn-out despite a high headline price?","trap_a":"The contingent portion may never be paid and the seller may lose control over decisions that determine the target metric.","es":"Earn-out difiere parte del precio y lo condiciona a resultados futuros; ayuda a cerrar gaps de valoración pero crea riesgo de ejecución.","related":["Contingent consideration","Valuation gap","SPA"]},{"id":"MA-ESCROW","t":"Escrow / holdback","cat":"M&A","sub":"Deal protection","f":3,"d":"Escrow or holdback retains part of seller proceeds temporarily to secure specified post-closing claims or adjustments.","formula":"Upfront cash to seller = price − escrow/holdback","why":"It gives the buyer a readily accessible remedy for agreed risks without chasing the seller later.","caveat":"It ties up seller proceeds and scope/duration must be negotiated carefully.","trap_q":"Is escrow the same as reducing headline purchase price?","trap_a":"Not necessarily. It may still be part of consideration, just withheld temporarily and released if claims do not arise.","es":"Escrow retiene parte del pago como protección ante reclamos o ajustes posteriores al cierre.","related":["Indemnity","Representations","Purchase price"]},{"id":"MA-RW","t":"Representations & warranties","cat":"M&A","sub":"Deal protection","f":4,"d":"Representations and warranties are contractual statements about the business and transaction facts, allocating risk if statements prove inaccurate.","formula":"No formula","why":"They support diligence and define potential remedies for undisclosed liabilities or factual breaches.","caveat":"Scope, knowledge qualifiers, materiality, survival and caps materially affect protection.","trap_q":"Why are reps not a substitute for diligence?","trap_a":"Because remedies may be capped, qualified or difficult to recover; diligence seeks to identify issues before signing.","es":"Representations & warranties son afirmaciones contractuales que reparten riesgo, pero no reemplazan investigar el negocio.","related":["Indemnity","Due diligence","R&W insurance"]},{"id":"MA-MAC","t":"Material adverse change clause","cat":"M&A","sub":"Deal protection","f":4,"d":"A MAC clause can permit termination or other consequences if a contractually defined severe adverse event occurs before closing, subject to negotiated exclusions.","formula":"No formula","why":"It allocates extreme interim business risk between signing and closing.","caveat":"Courts often interpret MAC clauses narrowly; ordinary deterioration usually does not qualify.","trap_q":"Can a buyer invoke MAC simply because quarterly earnings miss?","trap_a":"Usually not. The event generally must be materially adverse under the precise contract and legal standard.","es":"MAC cubre deterioros graves definidos contractualmente; no es una salida libre porque el negocio tenga un trimestre malo.","related":["Signing vs closing","Conditions","SPA"]},{"id":"MA-BREAK","t":"Break fee / reverse break fee","cat":"M&A","sub":"Deal protection","f":3,"d":"A break fee is payable if a deal terminates under specified circumstances; a reverse break fee is generally payable by the buyer in specified failure scenarios.","formula":"Fee often expressed as % of transaction equity value","why":"It compensates for deal failure risk and can influence commitment and bidder behaviour.","caveat":"Excessive fees can deter superior offers or face legal scrutiny.","trap_q":"What risk does a reverse break fee often address?","trap_a":"Buyer-side failure risk, such as financing or regulatory failure depending on the negotiated trigger.","es":"Break fees asignan parte del costo de que el deal falle; reverse break fee suele cubrir riesgo del comprador.","related":["Deal certainty","Financing condition","Regulatory risk"]},{"id":"MA-ASSETSTOCK","t":"Asset purchase vs stock purchase","cat":"M&A","sub":"Deal structure","f":5,"d":"In an asset purchase the buyer acquires selected assets/liabilities; in a stock purchase it acquires the legal entity’s shares and generally inherits its assets and liabilities.","formula":"Tax basis and liability transfer differ by structure","why":"Structure affects taxes, consents, liability assumption, depreciation/amortisation and execution complexity.","caveat":"Tax benefits to the buyer can create tax costs to the seller; legal consequences vary by jurisdiction.","trap_q":"Why might a buyer prefer an asset deal?","trap_a":"It may select liabilities and obtain tax basis step-ups, though transferring contracts, licences and employees can be more complex.","es":"Asset deal compra activos seleccionados; stock deal compra la entidad. Cambian impuestos, liabilities y complejidad.","related":["Tax step-up","Liabilities","Purchase accounting"]},{"id":"MA-CARVE","t":"Carve-out","cat":"M&A","sub":"Special situations","f":4,"d":"A carve-out separates and sells a business unit that may share systems, staff, contracts or infrastructure with the parent.","formula":"Standalone financials + separation costs + TSAs","why":"It can unlock value and sharpen strategic focus but requires building a standalone operating model.","caveat":"Historical segment financials may not reflect true standalone costs; stranded costs and dis-synergies are major risks.","trap_q":"Why can a carve-out’s reported EBITDA overstate standalone EBITDA?","trap_a":"Parent allocations may omit costs the business must incur independently, while stranded costs can remain with the seller.","es":"Carve-out exige separar un negocio integrado; el gran riesgo es que los estados históricos no reflejen costos standalone reales.","related":["Stranded costs","TSA","Divestiture"]},{"id":"MA-SPIN","t":"Spin-off","cat":"M&A","sub":"Special situations","f":3,"d":"A spin-off distributes shares of a subsidiary to the parent’s shareholders, creating an independent public company without a conventional sale.","formula":"Parent shareholders receive newco shares pro rata","why":"It can unlock strategic focus and valuation transparency without requiring a buyer.","caveat":"Separation costs, stranded costs, debt allocation and tax requirements can be significant.","trap_q":"Does a spin-off generate cash proceeds to the parent like a sale?","trap_a":"Not necessarily. It primarily distributes ownership; some structures can include debt or cash movements, but there is no external purchase price by default.","es":"Spin-off separa una subsidiaria y entrega sus acciones a los accionistas de la matriz; no es lo mismo que venderla.","related":["Carve-out","Divestiture","SOTP"]},{"id":"MA-JV","t":"Joint venture","cat":"M&A","sub":"Alternative structures","f":3,"d":"A JV is a jointly controlled business or arrangement formed by two or more parties to combine assets, capabilities or market access.","formula":"Ownership and economics defined by agreement","why":"It can share risk and investment while preserving strategic access without a full acquisition.","caveat":"Governance deadlock, uneven incentives and exit mechanisms can be difficult.","trap_q":"Why choose a JV instead of acquiring 100%?","trap_a":"To share capital/risk, access complementary capabilities, satisfy regulation or preserve partner incentives.","es":"JV comparte control, capital y riesgo entre socios; puede ser alternativa a comprar el 100%.","related":["Minority investment","Governance","Strategic alliance"]},{"id":"MA-MIN","t":"Minority investment","cat":"M&A","sub":"Alternative structures","f":3,"d":"A minority investment acquires less than controlling ownership and usually provides limited governance rights.","formula":"Ownership % < control threshold, subject to facts and rights","why":"It can create strategic exposure with lower capital commitment and preserve founder/partner incentives.","caveat":"Lack of control, liquidity and governance rights can limit the investor’s ability to protect value.","trap_q":"Can 40% ownership still provide control?","trap_a":"Potentially, depending on voting dispersion, contractual rights and governance; percentage alone does not determine control.","es":"Una inversión minoritaria reduce capital comprometido pero también limita control y liquidez.","related":["Control","JV","Equity method"]},{"id":"MA-ANTITRUST","t":"Antitrust / competition review","cat":"M&A","sub":"Regulatory","f":4,"d":"Competition authorities review whether a transaction may substantially lessen competition or harm consumers/markets under applicable law.","formula":"No formula","why":"Antitrust risk affects timing, remedies, deal certainty and acceptable transaction structure.","caveat":"Market definition and competitive effects are fact-specific; large size alone does not determine outcome.","trap_q":"Can a vertical merger face antitrust issues?","trap_a":"Yes. Authorities may consider foreclosure, access to inputs/customers or other competitive effects even without direct horizontal overlap.","es":"Antitrust evalúa efectos competitivos; no se limita a mergers horizontales ni a tamaño absoluto.","related":["Regulatory approval","Horizontal merger","Remedies"]},{"id":"MA-HOSTILE","t":"Hostile takeover","cat":"M&A","sub":"Public M&A","f":3,"d":"A hostile takeover is pursued without the target board’s support, often through a tender offer, proxy contest or public pressure.","formula":"No formula","why":"It changes tactics, information access, defence mechanisms and execution risk.","caveat":"Jurisdiction, shareholder structure and takeover rules heavily influence feasibility.","trap_q":"Can a hostile bidder conduct full private-company-style diligence before bidding?","trap_a":"Usually access is more limited because the target board is not cooperating, increasing information risk.","es":"En una toma hostil el board del target no apoya la oferta, por lo que cambian acceso a información y tácticas.","related":["Tender offer","Proxy contest","Defences"]},{"id":"MA-TENDER","t":"Tender offer","cat":"M&A","sub":"Public M&A","f":3,"d":"A tender offer is a public offer to shareholders to sell their shares at specified terms, often used in public acquisitions.","formula":"Offer price × shares tendered","why":"It can acquire shares directly from shareholders and may be used in friendly or hostile contexts depending on jurisdiction.","caveat":"Thresholds, timing, disclosure and financing rules are highly regulated.","trap_q":"Is every tender offer hostile?","trap_a":"No. Tender offers can be board-supported and friendly; “hostile” refers to lack of board support.","es":"Tender offer va directamente a accionistas bajo reglas públicas específicas; puede ser friendly o hostile.","related":["Hostile takeover","Public M&A","Offer premium"]},{"id":"CF-NPV","t":"Net present value","cat":"Corporate Finance","sub":"Capital budgeting","f":5,"d":"NPV is the present value of future incremental cash flows minus the initial investment.","formula":"NPV = Σ CF_t/(1+r)^t − Initial investment","why":"Positive NPV means an investment is expected to create value relative to the required return.","caveat":"NPV depends on forecast cash flows and the correct risk-adjusted discount rate; accounting earnings are not a substitute for cash flow.","trap_q":"Project A has higher IRR but lower NPV than Project B. Which creates more dollar value?","trap_a":"Project B creates more absolute value if its NPV is higher, assuming mutually exclusive projects and correctly risk-adjusted cash flows.","es":"NPV mide creación de valor en dinero presente; si es positivo, el proyecto supera el retorno requerido.","related":["IRR","Discount rate","Capital budgeting"]},{"id":"CF-IRR","t":"Internal rate of return","cat":"Corporate Finance","sub":"Capital budgeting","f":5,"d":"IRR is the discount rate that sets a project’s NPV to zero.","formula":"0 = Σ CF_t/(1+IRR)^t − Initial investment","why":"It expresses project return as a percentage and is common in private equity and capital budgeting.","caveat":"Non-conventional cash flows can create multiple IRRs; IRR can favour smaller or faster-payback projects over higher-NPV alternatives.","trap_q":"Can a project with a 40% IRR create less value than one with 20% IRR?","trap_a":"Yes. The 20% project may be much larger and generate more NPV; IRR measures rate of return, not absolute value.","es":"IRR es la tasa que hace NPV igual a cero; es útil pero no sustituye NPV para medir valor absoluto.","related":["NPV","MOIC","LBO"]},{"id":"CF-ROIC","t":"Return on invested capital","cat":"Corporate Finance","sub":"Returns","f":5,"d":"ROIC measures after-tax operating profit generated per unit of operating capital invested.","formula":"ROIC ≈ NOPAT / Average Invested Capital","why":"Comparing ROIC with WACC indicates whether growth is creating or destroying value at the operating level.","caveat":"Definitions of invested capital vary and cyclical or acquisition-heavy businesses can distort both numerator and denominator.","trap_q":"Revenue grows 20% but ROIC falls below WACC. Is that necessarily good growth?","trap_a":"No. Growth that earns below the cost of capital can destroy value even if revenue and EBITDA rise.","es":"ROIC compara NOPAT con capital operativo invertido; crecer por debajo de WACC puede destruir valor.","related":["WACC","NOPAT","Value creation"]},{"id":"CF-ROE","t":"Return on equity","cat":"Corporate Finance","sub":"Returns","f":5,"d":"ROE measures net income attributable to common shareholders relative to average common equity.","formula":"ROE = Net Income / Average Common Equity","why":"It indicates accounting return on shareholder capital and is especially relevant for financial institutions.","caveat":"ROE can rise because of leverage, buybacks or a smaller equity base, not only better operating performance.","trap_q":"ROE rises from 12% to 20%. Is that automatically positive?","trap_a":"No. Decompose the change: higher margins/asset turnover may be good, but higher leverage, one-offs or a shrinking equity base can also drive it.","es":"ROE mide utilidad sobre equity, pero puede subir por leverage o buybacks sin mejora operativa.","related":["DuPont","Leverage","ROTE"]},{"id":"CF-ROA","t":"Return on assets","cat":"Corporate Finance","sub":"Returns","f":3,"d":"ROA measures profit generated relative to the asset base.","formula":"ROA = Net Income / Average Total Assets","why":"It can be useful for comparing asset utilisation, particularly in banks and asset-heavy sectors.","caveat":"Asset accounting and leverage differ across sectors, making cross-industry comparisons weak.","trap_q":"Why can a bank have low ROA but acceptable ROE?","trap_a":"Banks operate with substantial leverage, so a modest return on a large asset base can translate into a higher return on equity.","es":"ROA mide utilidad sobre activos; en bancos suele ser bajo porque el balance es muy grande y apalancado.","related":["ROE","Asset turnover","Banks"]},{"id":"CF-ROCE","t":"Return on capital employed","cat":"Corporate Finance","sub":"Returns","f":3,"d":"ROCE measures operating profit relative to long-term capital employed and is often used for capital-intensive businesses.","formula":"ROCE ≈ EBIT / (Total Assets − Current Liabilities)","why":"It evaluates how efficiently a company earns operating profit from capital committed to the business.","caveat":"Definitions vary and leased assets, goodwill and cash treatment can change comparability.","trap_q":"Why might ROCE differ materially from ROIC?","trap_a":"Different numerators, tax treatment and definitions of the capital base can produce different results even for the same company.","es":"ROCE usa EBIT sobre capital empleado; se parece a ROIC pero no es idéntico en definición ni tax treatment.","related":["ROIC","EBIT","Capital employed"]},{"id":"CF-DUPONT","t":"DuPont analysis","cat":"Corporate Finance","sub":"Returns","f":4,"d":"DuPont decomposes ROE into profitability, efficiency and leverage drivers.","formula":"ROE = Net Margin × Asset Turnover × Financial Leverage","why":"It shows whether shareholder returns come from margins, asset utilisation or leverage.","caveat":"Accounting definitions and one-offs can distort each component; leverage-driven ROE is not necessarily sustainable.","trap_q":"Two firms have the same ROE. Can their economics be very different?","trap_a":"Yes. One may have high margins and low leverage; the other low margins but high turnover or leverage.","es":"DuPont separa ROE en margen, rotación de activos y leverage para entender qué realmente impulsa el retorno.","related":["ROE","Asset turnover","Financial leverage"]},{"id":"CF-OPLEV","t":"Operating leverage","cat":"Corporate Finance","sub":"Leverage","f":4,"d":"Operating leverage is sensitivity of operating profit to changes in revenue caused by fixed versus variable operating costs.","formula":"Degree of operating leverage ≈ %ΔEBIT / %ΔRevenue","why":"High fixed-cost businesses can see EBIT grow faster than revenue in upturns and fall faster in downturns.","caveat":"The degree changes with capacity utilisation and the current revenue base; it is not a fixed company constant.","trap_q":"Which is more cyclical: high or low operating leverage, all else equal?","trap_a":"High operating leverage usually amplifies profit sensitivity to volume, increasing cyclicality.","es":"Operating leverage viene de costos fijos: pequeños cambios en ventas pueden producir cambios mayores en EBIT.","related":["Fixed costs","Margins","Cyclicality"]},{"id":"CF-FINLEV","t":"Financial leverage","cat":"Corporate Finance","sub":"Leverage","f":5,"d":"Financial leverage uses debt or other fixed claims to increase the sensitivity of equity returns to enterprise performance.","formula":"Equity return ≈ enterprise return magnified by debt structure","why":"Leverage can enhance equity returns when enterprise returns exceed financing cost but also magnifies downside and default risk.","caveat":"More leverage is not free value: debt capacity, covenants, refinancing and volatility constrain it.","trap_q":"Why can leverage increase IRR without increasing enterprise value?","trap_a":"Debt reduces the upfront equity cheque and transfers part of financing to lenders, so the same enterprise outcome is earned on less equity capital.","es":"Financial leverage magnifica retorno y pérdida del equity porque la deuda es una obligación prioritaria.","related":["LBO","Debt","Equity risk"]},{"id":"CF-MM","t":"Modigliani–Miller intuition","cat":"Corporate Finance","sub":"Capital structure","f":3,"d":"In frictionless markets, changing the mix of debt and equity does not by itself change total enterprise value; real-world taxes, distress, agency costs and information make capital structure matter.","formula":"V_L = V_U in the no-tax ideal; with simple corporate tax, debt tax shields can add value","why":"The framework separates financing mechanics from true value drivers and clarifies why leverage is not magical.","caveat":"Real companies face bankruptcy costs, taxes, covenants and agency conflicts, so extreme leverage can destroy value.","trap_q":"If debt is cheaper than equity, why not fund a company with 100% debt?","trap_a":"Because debt cost rises with leverage, distress risk increases, lenders impose constraints and equity remains a residual loss-absorbing claim.","es":"MM muestra que deuda barata no crea valor gratis; en la realidad importan impuestos, distress y agency costs.","related":["WACC","Tax shield","Capital structure"]},{"id":"CF-TAXSH","t":"Interest tax shield","cat":"Corporate Finance","sub":"Capital structure","f":4,"d":"The interest tax shield is tax saved because deductible interest reduces taxable income.","formula":"Tax shield ≈ Interest expense × marginal tax rate","why":"It is one reason debt can lower after-tax financing cost and potentially add value.","caveat":"Deductibility limits, losses, jurisdiction and distress can reduce the realised shield.","trap_q":"A loss-making company takes on more debt. Is the tax shield immediately valuable?","trap_a":"Not necessarily. If it has no taxable income and cannot use deductions soon, the present value of the shield may be low.","es":"El tax shield es el ahorro fiscal por deducir intereses; solo vale si la empresa puede utilizar esa deducción.","related":["Cost of debt","WACC","NOLs"]},{"id":"CF-CURR","t":"Current ratio","cat":"Financial Ratios","sub":"Liquidity","f":3,"d":"Current ratio measures current assets relative to current liabilities.","formula":"Current Ratio = Current Assets / Current Liabilities","why":"It is a broad indicator of short-term balance-sheet liquidity.","caveat":"Inventory quality, receivable collectability and business-model working capital can make the ratio misleading.","trap_q":"Can a current ratio below 1 be healthy?","trap_a":"Yes. Supermarkets or subscription businesses can collect cash quickly and pay suppliers later, operating safely with structurally low working capital.","es":"Current ratio menor a 1 no siempre significa distress; algunos modelos tienen working capital negativo estructural.","related":["Quick ratio","NWC","Liquidity"]},{"id":"CF-QUICK","t":"Quick ratio","cat":"Financial Ratios","sub":"Liquidity","f":3,"d":"Quick ratio compares highly liquid current assets with current liabilities, generally excluding inventory.","formula":"Quick Ratio = (Cash + Marketable Securities + AR) / Current Liabilities","why":"It is stricter than current ratio when inventory may be slow-moving or hard to monetise.","caveat":"AR may still be uncollectible and the formula varies across analysts.","trap_q":"Why might a retailer have a weak quick ratio but no liquidity crisis?","trap_a":"Fast cash sales, rapid inventory turnover and supplier credit can support liquidity despite low quick assets.","es":"Quick ratio excluye inventario y es más estricto, pero debe interpretarse según el ciclo operativo.","related":["Current ratio","Cash ratio","Inventory"]},{"id":"CF-CCC","t":"Cash conversion cycle","cat":"Financial Ratios","sub":"Efficiency","f":5,"d":"CCC estimates days cash is tied up between paying suppliers and collecting customers.","formula":"CCC = DSO + DIO − DPO","why":"It combines receivables, inventory and payables efficiency into a working-capital measure.","caveat":"Negative CCC can be excellent for some models, while seasonality and accounting averages can distort the metric.","trap_q":"Can a negative CCC be a competitive advantage?","trap_a":"Yes. If customers pay before suppliers must be paid, growth can generate operating cash rather than consume it.","es":"CCC mide días de caja atrapada en el ciclo operativo; puede ser negativo y saludable.","related":["DSO","DIO","DPO"]},{"id":"CF-NDEBITDA","t":"Net debt / EBITDA","cat":"Financial Ratios","sub":"Leverage","f":5,"d":"Net debt/EBITDA measures net financial debt relative to a pre-interest earnings proxy.","formula":"Net Debt / EBITDA","why":"It is widely used for leverage, credit capacity and covenant analysis because it compares debt with operating earnings before capital structure.","caveat":"EBITDA is not cash flow, can be cyclically high and may ignore capex, leases or working-capital needs.","trap_q":"Two firms both at 4x net debt/EBITDA. Same credit risk?","trap_a":"No. Cash conversion, cyclicality, asset coverage, maturity profile, interest rates and business stability can make risk very different.","es":"Net debt/EBITDA es útil como leverage shortcut, pero no reemplaza analizar FCF, vencimientos y volatilidad.","related":["Interest coverage","Debt/FCF","Credit"]},{"id":"CF-ICOV","t":"Interest coverage","cat":"Financial Ratios","sub":"Leverage","f":5,"d":"Interest coverage measures earnings available to service interest expense, commonly EBIT/interest or EBITDA/interest.","formula":"Interest Coverage = EBIT or EBITDA / Interest Expense","why":"It gauges cushion before operating earnings become insufficient to cover interest.","caveat":"EBITDA coverage can overstate debt service capacity for capex-heavy businesses and ignores principal repayment.","trap_q":"A company has 5x EBITDA/interest but negative FCF. Safe?","trap_a":"Not automatically. Capex, working capital, taxes or principal maturities can create cash stress despite comfortable EBITDA coverage.","es":"Interest coverage mide margen para pagar intereses; usar EBITDA puede ser optimista si el negocio necesita mucho CapEx.","related":["DSCR","Net debt/EBITDA","Free cash flow"]},{"id":"CF-DSCR","t":"Debt service coverage ratio","cat":"Financial Ratios","sub":"Leverage","f":4,"d":"DSCR compares cash flow available for debt service with required interest and principal payments.","formula":"DSCR = Cash Flow Available for Debt Service / Debt Service","why":"It is useful where scheduled amortisation matters, such as project finance and certain credit structures.","caveat":"Definitions of cash flow and debt service vary by covenant and transaction.","trap_q":"Why can DSCR be more informative than interest coverage?","trap_a":"Because it includes principal repayment as well as interest, capturing the full scheduled cash burden.","es":"DSCR compara caja disponible con intereses y principal, por lo que refleja mejor la carga total de deuda.","related":["Interest coverage","Amortisation","Project finance"]},{"id":"CF-GM","t":"Gross margin","cat":"Financial Ratios","sub":"Profitability","f":4,"d":"Gross margin measures gross profit as a percentage of revenue.","formula":"Gross Margin = (Revenue − COGS) / Revenue","why":"It reflects pricing, product mix and direct input economics before operating overhead.","caveat":"Cost classification varies by company and sector; mix shifts can move margin without changing unit economics.","trap_q":"Revenue rises but gross margin falls sharply. What might be happening?","trap_a":"Input inflation, discounting, mix shift to lower-margin products, under-utilisation or competitive pricing pressure.","es":"Gross margin muestra cuánto queda después de costos directos y ayuda a analizar pricing power y mix.","related":["COGS","Pricing power","EBITDA margin"]},{"id":"CF-EBITDAM","t":"EBITDA margin","cat":"Financial Ratios","sub":"Profitability","f":4,"d":"EBITDA margin is EBITDA as a percentage of revenue.","formula":"EBITDA Margin = EBITDA / Revenue","why":"It captures operating profitability before D&A and financing and is useful for comparing cost structures.","caveat":"It can flatter capex-heavy businesses and is affected by classification of SBC, restructuring or lease expenses.","trap_q":"Company A has higher EBITDA margin than B. Is it necessarily better?","trap_a":"No. Growth, capex intensity, ROIC, cash conversion and accounting choices can make B economically superior.","es":"EBITDA margin compara rentabilidad operativa, pero debe leerse junto con reinversión y cash conversion.","related":["EBITDA","FCF margin","Operating leverage"]},{"id":"CF-FCFM","t":"Free cash flow margin","cat":"Financial Ratios","sub":"Cash flow","f":4,"d":"FCF margin measures free cash flow generated per unit of revenue.","formula":"FCF Margin = Free Cash Flow / Revenue","why":"It captures conversion of sales into discretionary cash after reinvestment.","caveat":"FCF is definition-dependent and can swing with working capital or one-time capex.","trap_q":"Why can FCF margin exceed net margin?","trap_a":"Large non-cash expenses, low capex in the period, favourable working-capital movements or tax timing can make cash flow exceed accounting profit.","es":"FCF margin mide conversión real de ventas a caja después de reinversión; puede diferir mucho del net margin.","related":["FCF","Cash conversion","Net margin"]},{"id":"CF-DSO","t":"Days sales outstanding","cat":"Financial Ratios","sub":"Efficiency","f":4,"d":"DSO estimates the average number of days needed to collect receivables.","formula":"DSO ≈ Average AR / Revenue × days","why":"It helps assess collection efficiency, customer terms and revenue quality.","caveat":"Seasonality, acquisitions and revenue mix can distort period-end calculations.","trap_q":"DSO increases while sales growth accelerates. Always bad?","trap_a":"No. It may reflect growth timing or deliberate longer terms, but it also warrants checking collections and revenue quality.","es":"DSO estima días de cobro; un aumento puede ser crecimiento/términos o una señal de deterioro.","related":["Accounts receivable","CCC","Revenue quality"]},{"id":"CF-DIO","t":"Days inventory outstanding","cat":"Financial Ratios","sub":"Efficiency","f":4,"d":"DIO estimates how many days inventory is held before sale.","formula":"DIO ≈ Average Inventory / COGS × days","why":"It measures inventory efficiency and can flag demand or supply-chain changes.","caveat":"Higher DIO can be intentional ahead of growth or shortages, not only obsolescence.","trap_q":"DIO rises materially before peak season. Red flag?","trap_a":"Not necessarily. Seasonal build can be rational; compare with historical seasonality and expected sales.","es":"DIO mide días de inventario; hay que distinguir build estratégico de inventario obsoleto.","related":["Inventory turnover","CCC","Working capital"]},{"id":"CF-DPO","t":"Days payables outstanding","cat":"Financial Ratios","sub":"Efficiency","f":4,"d":"DPO estimates the average number of days a company takes to pay suppliers.","formula":"DPO ≈ Average AP / COGS × days","why":"It measures supplier financing and is a component of the cash conversion cycle.","caveat":"Higher DPO improves cash temporarily but may reflect distress or damage supplier relationships.","trap_q":"DPO jumps abruptly while liquidity weakens. Positive working-capital management?","trap_a":"Possibly, but it can also signal that the company is stretching suppliers because it lacks cash.","es":"DPO alto conserva caja, pero un salto brusco puede ser señal de tensión de liquidez.","related":["Accounts payable","CCC","Liquidity"]},{"id":"CF-CASHCONV","t":"EBITDA to cash conversion","cat":"Financial Ratios","sub":"Cash flow","f":4,"d":"Cash conversion measures how much accounting operating earnings translate into operating or free cash flow.","formula":"Examples: OCF/EBITDA or FCF/EBITDA","why":"It helps distinguish high-quality earnings from businesses that consume cash in working capital or capex.","caveat":"Ratios can be volatile because timing of taxes, working capital and one-off capex shifts between periods.","trap_q":"Net income rises while OCF falls. What would you investigate?","trap_a":"AR and inventory growth, lower AP, non-cash gains, revenue recognition, tax payments and other working-capital changes.","es":"Cash conversion ayuda a ver si EBITDA y utilidad realmente llegan a caja.","related":["Earnings quality","Working capital","FCF"]},{"id":"CF-SHARPE","t":"Sharpe ratio","cat":"Financial Ratios","sub":"Investment performance","f":4,"d":"Sharpe ratio measures excess return per unit of total return volatility.","formula":"Sharpe = (Portfolio Return − Risk-Free Rate) / Standard Deviation","why":"It allows risk-adjusted comparison when total volatility is an appropriate risk proxy.","caveat":"It treats upside and downside volatility equally and can understate tail or non-normal risks.","trap_q":"Can a high Sharpe hide tail risk?","trap_a":"Yes. Strategies with small steady gains and rare severe losses can show attractive historical Sharpe ratios.","es":"Sharpe usa volatilidad total; una estrategia puede verse estable y aun esconder pérdidas extremas raras.","related":["Sortino","Volatility","Tail risk"]},{"id":"CF-SORTINO","t":"Sortino ratio","cat":"Financial Ratios","sub":"Investment performance","f":4,"d":"Sortino ratio measures excess return relative to downside deviation rather than total volatility.","formula":"Sortino = (Return − Target/Minimum Acceptable Return) / Downside Deviation","why":"It avoids penalising beneficial upside volatility and focuses on harmful dispersion below a target.","caveat":"It depends on target definition and still may not capture extreme tail risk or liquidity.","trap_q":"Why use Sortino instead of Sharpe?","trap_a":"When downside volatility is more relevant than total volatility and upside variability should not be treated as a penalty.","es":"Sortino penaliza solo downside deviation, por eso distingue volatilidad negativa de movimientos positivos.","related":["Sharpe","Downside deviation","Calmar"]},{"id":"CF-INFO","t":"Information ratio","cat":"Financial Ratios","sub":"Investment performance","f":3,"d":"Information ratio measures active return relative to active risk, or tracking error, versus a benchmark.","formula":"IR = (Portfolio Return − Benchmark Return) / Tracking Error","why":"It evaluates consistency of benchmark-relative alpha generation.","caveat":"Benchmark choice and short samples can make the metric misleading; it does not measure absolute risk.","trap_q":"Fund beats benchmark by 2% with 1% tracking error. Approximate IR?","trap_a":"About 2.0, assuming those figures are measured consistently over the same horizon.","es":"Information ratio mide retorno activo por unidad de tracking error frente a un benchmark.","related":["Tracking error","Alpha","Sharpe"]},{"id":"LBO-MECH","t":"LBO mechanics","cat":"LBO / Credit","sub":"LBO","f":5,"d":"An LBO acquires a company using substantial debt plus sponsor equity, then seeks equity returns through EBITDA growth, debt paydown, cash generation and exit value.","formula":"Sponsor Equity = Uses − Debt & other sources; Exit Equity = Exit EV − Exit Net Debt","why":"Leverage reduces upfront equity and debt paydown transfers enterprise value to equity over time.","caveat":"Returns can be highly sensitive to exit multiple, operating performance and refinancing conditions.","trap_q":"Can an LBO produce a strong IRR with no EBITDA growth?","trap_a":"Yes, through debt paydown and/or multiple expansion, although reliance on multiple expansion is usually less defensible.","es":"LBO usa deuda para reducir el equity inicial; el retorno viene de crecimiento, paydown y exit multiple.","related":["Sources & uses","IRR","Debt paydown"]},{"id":"LBO-CAND","t":"Good LBO candidate","cat":"LBO / Credit","sub":"LBO","f":5,"d":"A good LBO candidate typically has stable cash flow, defensible margins, manageable capex, working-capital predictability, debt capacity and credible exit options.","formula":"Debt capacity depends on cash flow and downside resilience","why":"Predictability supports leverage and debt repayment while operational upside can enhance returns.","caveat":"Cheap valuation alone is insufficient if cash flow is volatile or the business needs heavy reinvestment.","trap_q":"Why are highly cyclical businesses harder LBO candidates?","trap_a":"Debt service is fixed while earnings can collapse in downturns, increasing default and refinancing risk.","es":"Un buen LBO necesita caja predecible y capacidad de deuda, no solo un múltiplo de entrada bajo.","related":["Leverage","Cash flow","Exit"]},{"id":"LBO-MOIC","t":"MOIC","cat":"LBO / Credit","sub":"Returns","f":5,"d":"MOIC is total equity proceeds divided by invested equity capital.","formula":"MOIC = Equity Proceeds / Invested Equity","why":"It measures absolute multiple of money returned regardless of time.","caveat":"It ignores holding period, so two identical MOICs can have very different annualised returns.","trap_q":"2.0x MOIC in 3 years versus 2.0x in 8 years: same return?","trap_a":"Same multiple of money, but the 3-year investment has a much higher IRR because capital is returned sooner.","es":"MOIC mide cuántas veces recuperas el equity; no incorpora tiempo.","related":["IRR","Holding period","Private equity"]},{"id":"LBO-IRR","t":"LBO IRR drivers","cat":"LBO / Credit","sub":"Returns","f":5,"d":"LBO IRR is driven by entry valuation, operating performance, leverage, debt paydown, exit valuation and holding period.","formula":"IRR solves PV of equity cash flows = 0","why":"It captures annualised sponsor return and is a core PE decision metric.","caveat":"High leverage can inflate base-case IRR while increasing downside probability; short holding periods can also mechanically raise IRR.","trap_q":"Which improves IRR more: same cash proceeds in year 4 or year 6?","trap_a":"Year 4, because earlier realisation increases the annualised return.","es":"IRR en LBO depende no solo de cuánto ganas, sino de cuándo y con cuánto equity inicial.","related":["MOIC","Entry multiple","Exit multiple"]},{"id":"LBO-DEBTPAY","t":"Debt paydown","cat":"LBO / Credit","sub":"LBO","f":5,"d":"Debt paydown uses free cash flow to reduce debt, increasing the residual equity value for a given enterprise value.","formula":"Exit Equity = Exit EV − Exit Net Debt","why":"It is a major LBO value-creation mechanism even without multiple expansion.","caveat":"Paydown requires actual cash generation; EBITDA growth that does not convert to cash may not reduce debt.","trap_q":"EV is unchanged but debt falls by $100. What happens to equity value?","trap_a":"All else equal, equity value rises by $100 because the senior debt claim is $100 lower.","es":"Si EV no cambia y la deuda baja, el residual para equity sube dólar por dólar.","related":["Exit equity","FCF","Leverage"]},{"id":"LBO-PIK","t":"PIK interest","cat":"LBO / Credit","sub":"Debt structure","f":4,"d":"PIK interest accrues to principal rather than being paid in cash currently.","formula":"Ending debt = Beginning debt + PIK interest − repayments","why":"It preserves near-term liquidity but increases future debt balance and leverage.","caveat":"Compounding can become expensive and increase refinancing/default risk.","trap_q":"Does PIK improve free cash flow today?","trap_a":"Cash flow is higher than with cash interest, but debt grows, so economic cost is deferred rather than eliminated.","es":"PIK evita salida de caja hoy, pero capitaliza intereses y aumenta deuda futura.","related":["Mezzanine","Cash interest","Debt balance"]},{"id":"LBO-CASHSWEEP","t":"Cash sweep","cat":"LBO / Credit","sub":"Debt structure","f":3,"d":"A cash sweep requires or allows excess cash flow to repay debt ahead of scheduled amortisation.","formula":"Excess cash applied to debt principal","why":"It accelerates deleveraging and reduces interest expense, supporting equity returns and lender protection.","caveat":"It reduces sponsor flexibility to retain cash for acquisitions, dividends or other uses.","trap_q":"Why do lenders like cash sweeps?","trap_a":"They shorten risk duration and reduce leverage faster when the business performs well.","es":"Cash sweep usa exceso de caja para amortizar deuda antes, reduciendo riesgo y leverage.","related":["Debt paydown","Covenants","FCF"]},{"id":"LBO-DIVREC","t":"Dividend recapitalisation","cat":"LBO / Credit","sub":"LBO","f":4,"d":"A dividend recap raises additional debt to fund a dividend to the sponsor before exit.","formula":"New debt proceeds → sponsor dividend","why":"It can return capital early and boost IRR while the sponsor retains ownership.","caveat":"It increases leverage and can weaken credit quality; lenders and markets may resist.","trap_q":"Does a dividend recap create enterprise value?","trap_a":"No. It changes financing and distributes cash to equity; it can raise sponsor IRR but also increases debt risk.","es":"Dividend recap devuelve capital al sponsor con nueva deuda; mejora IRR pero no crea EV por sí mismo.","related":["IRR","Leverage","Debt"]},{"id":"CR-COV","t":"Covenants","cat":"LBO / Credit","sub":"Credit","f":5,"d":"Covenants are contractual restrictions or tests designed to protect lenders and shape borrower behaviour.","formula":"Maintenance tests and incurrence tests use leverage, coverage or other metrics","why":"They can trigger remedies before full payment default and constrain leverage, distributions or acquisitions.","caveat":"Definitions are heavily negotiated and add-backs can materially change covenant calculations.","trap_q":"Maintenance vs incurrence covenant?","trap_a":"Maintenance is tested regularly regardless of action; incurrence is tested when the borrower takes a specified action such as new debt or a dividend.","es":"Covenants protegen al lender con límites o tests; hay que leer exactamente definiciones y add-backs.","related":["Leverage ratio","Default","Credit agreement"]},{"id":"CR-PD","t":"Probability of default","cat":"LBO / Credit","sub":"Credit risk","f":4,"d":"PD is the probability that a borrower defaults over a specified horizon under a defined default standard.","formula":"Expected Loss ≈ PD × LGD × EAD","why":"It is a core component of credit pricing, expected loss and risk management.","caveat":"PD depends on horizon and scenario and is not the same as expected percentage loss.","trap_q":"A loan has 5% PD. Does that mean expected loss is 5%?","trap_a":"No. Expected loss also depends on exposure and recovery/LGD; a default may not produce a total loss.","es":"PD es probabilidad de default, no porcentaje de pérdida esperado.","related":["LGD","Expected loss","Rating"]},{"id":"CR-LGD","t":"Loss given default","cat":"LBO / Credit","sub":"Credit risk","f":4,"d":"LGD is the percentage of exposure expected to be lost if default occurs, after recoveries.","formula":"LGD = 1 − Recovery Rate","why":"It reflects collateral, seniority, enterprise value and workout outcomes and is central to expected credit loss.","caveat":"Recoveries are uncertain and vary through the cycle and by instrument structure.","trap_q":"Senior secured and subordinated debt of same issuer: same LGD?","trap_a":"Usually not. Senior secured debt generally has better claim priority/collateral and therefore lower expected LGD.","es":"LGD mide cuánto se pierde si ocurre default; depende de recovery, collateral y seniority.","related":["Recovery rate","Seniority","Collateral"]},{"id":"CR-EL","t":"Expected credit loss","cat":"LBO / Credit","sub":"Credit risk","f":4,"d":"Expected loss combines default probability, loss severity and exposure.","formula":"EL = PD × LGD × EAD","why":"It separates frequency and severity of credit loss and supports pricing/provisioning frameworks.","caveat":"Tail outcomes and unexpected loss require additional capital/risk analysis beyond expected loss.","trap_q":"PD doubles but collateral improves enough to halve LGD. What happens to EL, all else equal?","trap_a":"It is roughly unchanged because the two effects offset multiplicatively.","es":"Expected loss combina probabilidad, severidad y exposición; no depende de una sola métrica.","related":["PD","LGD","EAD"]},{"id":"CR-SPREAD","t":"Credit spread","cat":"LBO / Credit","sub":"Credit markets","f":5,"d":"Credit spread is the yield premium over a benchmark or comparable risk-free curve demanded for credit, liquidity and other risks.","formula":"Bond yield ≈ risk-free/benchmark yield + credit spread","why":"Spread changes reveal market perception of default risk, liquidity and risk appetite.","caveat":"Spread is not pure expected default loss; it includes risk premium, liquidity and technical factors.","trap_q":"Risk-free yields fall but corporate bond yield stays flat. What happened to spread?","trap_a":"The credit spread widened by roughly the amount the risk-free yield fell.","es":"Credit spread es la prima sobre una curva benchmark; incluye default risk, liquidez y risk premium.","related":["Bond yield","OAS","Default risk"]},{"id":"CR-SEN","t":"Seniority & security","cat":"LBO / Credit","sub":"Credit structure","f":4,"d":"Seniority determines claim priority in insolvency; security gives lenders claims over specified collateral.","formula":"Recovery generally improves with higher priority and collateral, all else equal","why":"It affects expected recovery, pricing and covenant protection.","caveat":"Actual recovery depends on collateral value, intercreditor terms, jurisdiction and enterprise value at default.","trap_q":"Can unsecured debt ever recover more than secured debt?","trap_a":"In unusual structures or outcomes yes, but all else equal senior secured claims generally have better recovery prospects.","es":"Seniority define prioridad; security añade collateral. Ambos suelen mejorar recovery, pero no garantizan resultado.","related":["Recovery","LGD","Intercreditor"]},{"id":"CR-RATING","t":"Credit rating","cat":"LBO / Credit","sub":"Credit risk","f":3,"d":"A credit rating is an agency opinion on relative creditworthiness of an issuer or instrument.","formula":"No formula","why":"Ratings influence investor mandates, funding costs, collateral rules and market access.","caveat":"Ratings are opinions, can lag markets and are not guarantees of default probability.","trap_q":"A downgrade occurs. What usually happens to bond price and yield, all else equal?","trap_a":"Price tends to fall and yield/spread rise as required compensation for perceived risk increases, though market pricing may have anticipated it.","es":"Un downgrade normalmente presiona precio a la baja y yield/spread al alza, pero el mercado puede haberlo descontado antes.","related":["Credit spread","Bond price","Default risk"]},{"id":"FI-PRICE","t":"Bond pricing","cat":"Fixed Income / Derivatives","sub":"Fixed income","f":5,"d":"A bond price is the present value of future coupon and principal cash flows discounted at required yields.","formula":"Price = Σ Coupon_t/(1+y)^t + Principal/(1+y)^n","why":"It explains the inverse relationship between required yield and fixed bond price.","caveat":"A single YTM assumes a common discount rate; spot curves are more precise for distinct cash-flow maturities.","trap_q":"Why do bond prices fall when yields rise?","trap_a":"The same fixed cash flows are discounted at a higher required return, reducing present value.","es":"Precio y yield se mueven inversamente porque los cash flows fijos se descuentan a una tasa requerida distinta.","related":["YTM","Duration","Spot rates"]},{"id":"FI-YTM","t":"Yield to maturity","cat":"Fixed Income / Derivatives","sub":"Fixed income","f":5,"d":"YTM is the single discount rate that equates a bond’s market price with the present value of promised cash flows if held to maturity under standard assumptions.","formula":"Price = PV of coupons and principal discounted at YTM","why":"It summarises bond valuation and return into one rate.","caveat":"It assumes promised payments and reinvestment at YTM; it is not guaranteed realised return.","trap_q":"Bond trades at a premium to par. Coupon rate versus YTM?","trap_a":"For a standard fixed-rate bond, coupon rate is generally above YTM when price is above par.","es":"YTM es la tasa que iguala precio con cash flows prometidos; no garantiza el retorno realizado.","related":["Coupon","Current yield","Bond price"]},{"id":"FI-CLEAN","t":"Clean vs dirty price","cat":"Fixed Income / Derivatives","sub":"Fixed income","f":4,"d":"Clean price excludes accrued interest; dirty price includes it and is typically the cash settlement amount per unit.","formula":"Dirty Price = Clean Price + Accrued Interest","why":"It separates quoted market price movement from coupon accrual between payment dates.","caveat":"Day-count and ex-coupon conventions affect accrued interest.","trap_q":"A bond trades clean at 101 and accrued interest is 1.5. Dirty price?","trap_a":"102.5 per 100 of face value, before other settlement adjustments.","es":"Clean excluye accrued interest; dirty lo incluye y representa mejor el importe de settlement.","related":["Accrued interest","Settlement","Coupon"]},{"id":"FI-DUR","t":"Duration","cat":"Fixed Income / Derivatives","sub":"Fixed income","f":5,"d":"Duration measures interest-rate sensitivity and, in Macaulay form, the weighted average timing of bond cash flows.","formula":"Approx %ΔP ≈ −Modified Duration × ΔYield","why":"Modified duration provides a first-order estimate of price response to small yield changes.","caveat":"It is a linear approximation and becomes less accurate for large yield moves, embedded options or curve-shape changes.","trap_q":"Bond duration is 6. Yield rises 50 bps. Approx price move?","trap_a":"Approximately −3% before convexity: −6 × 0.005 = −0.03.","es":"Duration aproxima sensibilidad de precio a yield; una duration de 6 implica ~6% por 100 bps para movimientos pequeños.","related":["Modified duration","Convexity","DV01"]},{"id":"FI-CONV","t":"Convexity","cat":"Fixed Income / Derivatives","sub":"Fixed income","f":4,"d":"Convexity measures curvature in the bond price-yield relationship and improves duration-based estimates for larger rate moves.","formula":"%ΔP ≈ −DmodΔy + 0.5×Convexity×(Δy)^2","why":"Positive convexity means gains from falling yields tend to exceed losses from equal yield increases relative to a linear estimate.","caveat":"Callable bonds can exhibit negative convexity when falling yields increase call probability.","trap_q":"Why does convexity matter more for large yield moves?","trap_a":"Because the price-yield relationship is curved; the second-order term grows with the square of the yield change.","es":"Convexity corrige la aproximación lineal de duration y se vuelve más importante cuando los yields se mueven mucho.","related":["Duration","Callable bonds","DV01"]},{"id":"FI-DV01","t":"DV01","cat":"Fixed Income / Derivatives","sub":"Fixed income","f":4,"d":"DV01 estimates the dollar price change for a one-basis-point parallel yield move.","formula":"DV01 ≈ Modified Duration × Price × 0.0001","why":"It converts duration into a practical monetary risk measure for hedging and position sizing.","caveat":"It is local and assumes a particular curve move; key-rate DV01 may be needed for non-parallel shifts.","trap_q":"$10m bond position, duration 5, price near par. Rough DV01?","trap_a":"About $5,000: 10,000,000 × 5 × 0.0001.","es":"DV01 expresa en dinero cuánto cambia el valor por 1 bp de movimiento de yield.","related":["Duration","Key-rate duration","Hedging"]},{"id":"FI-CURVE","t":"Yield curve","cat":"Fixed Income / Derivatives","sub":"Rates","f":5,"d":"A yield curve plots yields across maturities for comparable credit quality, commonly government securities or swap rates.","formula":"Term structure: yield as a function of maturity","why":"Its shape reflects expected policy rates, inflation, growth, term premium and supply/demand.","caveat":"A curve is not a pure forecast of future short rates because term premium and technical factors matter.","trap_q":"Why can the curve invert?","trap_a":"Markets may expect future policy easing or weaker growth, and term premia can be compressed; inversion is not a mechanical recession timer.","es":"La curva refleja expectativas y term premium; una inversión sugiere tasas cortas altas respecto a largas, pero no es una ley mecánica.","related":["Forward rates","Term premium","Monetary policy"]},{"id":"FI-SPOT","t":"Spot rates","cat":"Fixed Income / Derivatives","sub":"Rates","f":4,"d":"A spot rate is the zero-coupon yield for a single maturity and discounts one cash flow at that horizon.","formula":"PV(CF_t)=CF_t/(1+s_t)^t","why":"Spot curves allow each bond cash flow to be discounted at the appropriate maturity-specific rate.","caveat":"Bootstrapping depends on instrument conventions and curve construction choices.","trap_q":"Why can two bonds with same YTM have different fair values under a spot curve?","trap_a":"Their cash-flow timing differs, so exposure to maturity-specific spot rates differs even if one summary YTM happens to match.","es":"Spot rates descuentan cada maturity de forma específica y son más precisas que usar un único YTM.","related":["Zero coupon","Forward rate","Bootstrapping"]},{"id":"FI-FWD","t":"Forward rates","cat":"Fixed Income / Derivatives","sub":"Rates","f":4,"d":"Forward rates are rates implied today for borrowing/lending over a future period, derived from spot rates under no-arbitrage.","formula":"(1+s_n)^n = (1+s_m)^m × (1+f_{m,n})^(n−m)","why":"They translate the current curve into future-period pricing and underpin swaps and forwards.","caveat":"Implied forwards are not unbiased forecasts because risk premia and technical factors are embedded.","trap_q":"Is a 1y1y forward the market’s guaranteed forecast of the one-year rate in one year?","trap_a":"No. It is a no-arbitrage implied rate that also embeds risk premia and market pricing.","es":"Forward rate es una tasa implícita por no-arbitraje, no una predicción garantizada.","related":["Spot rates","Yield curve","Swaps"]},{"id":"FI-OAS","t":"Option-adjusted spread","cat":"Fixed Income / Derivatives","sub":"Credit","f":4,"d":"OAS is the spread over a benchmark curve after adjusting for the value of embedded options.","formula":"Model price using benchmark curve + OAS while simulating option exercise","why":"It improves comparison of callable, puttable or mortgage securities where option value affects nominal spread.","caveat":"OAS is model-dependent and sensitive to interest-rate volatility assumptions.","trap_q":"Why can a callable bond’s nominal spread look high?","trap_a":"Part of the extra yield compensates the investor for selling the call option to the issuer; OAS attempts to strip that option effect.","es":"OAS intenta separar credit/liquidity spread del valor de opciones embebidas.","related":["Z-spread","Callable bond","MBS"]},{"id":"FI-MBS","t":"Mortgage-backed securities","cat":"Fixed Income / Derivatives","sub":"Securitisation","f":4,"d":"MBS are securities backed by pools of mortgage cash flows, exposing investors to credit and borrower prepayment behaviour.","formula":"Investor cash flow depends on mortgage principal and interest pass-through","why":"They transform mortgage assets into tradable securities and create duration/prepayment risk.","caveat":"Prepayments make cash-flow timing uncertain and can create negative convexity.","trap_q":"Why can falling rates hurt an MBS investor relative to a non-callable bond?","trap_a":"Borrowers refinance and prepay, returning principal when yields are low and shortening the investor’s higher-coupon asset.","es":"MBS tiene prepayment risk: cuando bajan tasas, prestatarios refinancian y el inversor recibe principal antes.","related":["Prepayment risk","Negative convexity","CMO"]},{"id":"FI-TRANCHE","t":"Securitisation tranches","cat":"Fixed Income / Derivatives","sub":"Securitisation","f":4,"d":"Tranches divide a securitisation’s cash flows and losses into classes with different priority, risk and return.","formula":"Waterfall allocates cash and losses by contractual seniority","why":"Senior tranches receive protection from subordinated tranches, allowing risk to be redistributed.","caveat":"Correlation and tail losses can overwhelm subordination; ratings depend heavily on structure and assumptions.","trap_q":"Does a senior tranche mean zero risk?","trap_a":"No. It has priority and credit enhancement, but severe collateral losses or structural weaknesses can still cause loss.","es":"Las tranches redistribuyen riesgo mediante prioridad de pagos y absorción de pérdidas.","related":["Waterfall","Credit enhancement","ABS"]},{"id":"DER-FWD","t":"Forward contract","cat":"Fixed Income / Derivatives","sub":"Derivatives","f":4,"d":"A forward is an OTC agreement to exchange an asset at a future date for a price fixed today.","formula":"Long payoff at maturity = S_T − K","why":"It locks future price exposure and is customisable.","caveat":"OTC forwards create counterparty risk and are less standardised/liquid than exchange-traded futures.","trap_q":"At inception, is a fairly priced forward worth the notional amount?","trap_a":"No. A standard forward is typically initiated at near-zero value; notional is reference exposure, not upfront value.","es":"Forward fija hoy un precio futuro; el notional no es lo mismo que el valor del contrato.","related":["Futures","Forward price","Counterparty risk"]},{"id":"DER-FUT","t":"Futures contract","cat":"Fixed Income / Derivatives","sub":"Derivatives","f":4,"d":"A futures contract is a standardised exchange-traded forward-like contract subject to daily marking-to-market and margin.","formula":"Daily P&L = price change × contract exposure","why":"Futures reduce bilateral counterparty risk through clearing and standardisation.","caveat":"Daily margin creates liquidity needs and futures may not perfectly match the hedged exposure.","trap_q":"Forward and future with same maturity always identical price?","trap_a":"Not necessarily. Daily settlement and correlation between rates and underlying prices can create differences.","es":"Futures se marcan a mercado diariamente y usan margin/clearing, a diferencia de forwards OTC.","related":["Forward","Margin","Clearing"]},{"id":"DER-OPT","t":"Options","cat":"Fixed Income / Derivatives","sub":"Derivatives","f":5,"d":"An option gives the buyer the right, not obligation, to buy or sell an underlying at a strike by or at expiry depending on style.","formula":"Call payoff=max(S−K,0); Put payoff=max(K−S,0)","why":"Options create asymmetric payoffs and separate downside protection from upside participation.","caveat":"Premium can be lost entirely and valuation depends on volatility, time, rates, dividends and underlying price.","trap_q":"Can an out-of-the-money option have positive value?","trap_a":"Yes. It can have time value because future price moves may put it in the money before expiry.","es":"Una opción OTM puede valer más que cero por time value y probabilidad de terminar ITM.","related":["Intrinsic value","Time value","Implied volatility"]},{"id":"DER-GREEKS","t":"Option Greeks","cat":"Fixed Income / Derivatives","sub":"Derivatives","f":5,"d":"Greeks measure option-value sensitivity to underlying price, curvature, volatility, time and rates.","formula":"Delta=dV/dS; Gamma=d²V/dS²; Vega=dV/dσ; Theta=dV/dt","why":"They decompose nonlinear option risk and guide hedging.","caveat":"Greeks are local sensitivities that change as market conditions and time evolve.","trap_q":"Why can a delta-neutral option book still lose money on a large move?","trap_a":"Gamma, vega, skew and discrete hedging matter; delta neutrality is only first-order and local.","es":"Greeks son sensibilidades locales; estar delta-neutral no elimina gamma, vega ni otros riesgos.","related":["Delta","Gamma","Vega"]},{"id":"DER-IV","t":"Implied volatility","cat":"Fixed Income / Derivatives","sub":"Derivatives","f":4,"d":"Implied volatility is the volatility input that makes an option-pricing model equal the observed market price.","formula":"Observed option price → solve model for σ","why":"It summarises the market price of option uncertainty and is central to relative-value analysis.","caveat":"It is model-implied, not a direct forecast, and varies by strike and maturity through skew/surface.","trap_q":"Option price rises while spot, time and rates are unchanged. What could explain it?","trap_a":"Higher implied volatility or another unobserved pricing input such as dividends/skew effects.","es":"Implied vol es la volatilidad que hace que el modelo reproduzca el precio de mercado; no es una predicción pura.","related":["Realised volatility","Vega","Volatility surface"]},{"id":"DER-SWAP","t":"Interest rate swap","cat":"Fixed Income / Derivatives","sub":"Derivatives","f":5,"d":"An interest-rate swap exchanges fixed and floating interest cash flows on a notional amount without exchanging that notional in a standard single-currency vanilla swap.","formula":"Fixed leg PV = Floating leg PV at inception","why":"It transforms rate exposure, allowing borrowers or investors to switch between fixed and floating economics.","caveat":"Counterparty, collateral, basis and curve risks remain; notional is not usually exchanged.","trap_q":"Company pays fixed and receives floating. What exposure does it synthetically create?","trap_a":"It benefits when floating rates rise relative to the fixed rate; combined with floating-rate debt it can synthetically fix borrowing cost.","es":"Un IRS intercambia pagos fixed y floating sobre un notional; el notional normalmente no se intercambia.","related":["SOFR","Fixed rate","Floating rate"]},{"id":"DER-FXSW","t":"FX swap","cat":"Fixed Income / Derivatives","sub":"Derivatives","f":4,"d":"An FX swap combines a spot currency exchange with a reverse exchange at a forward date.","formula":"Forward points reflect interest-rate differential under covered interest parity","why":"It is widely used for short-term currency funding and hedging without taking outright FX direction.","caveat":"Cross-currency basis and funding conditions can move pricing away from simple textbook parity relationships.","trap_q":"Is an FX swap the same as a cross-currency interest-rate swap?","trap_a":"No. An FX swap usually exchanges principal spot and reverses it forward; a cross-currency swap typically exchanges longer-dated interest streams and often principal.","es":"FX swap combina spot y forward inverso; se usa mucho para funding y hedging de moneda.","related":["FX forward","Cross-currency swap","Covered interest parity"]},{"id":"DER-CDS","t":"Credit default swap","cat":"Fixed Income / Derivatives","sub":"Credit derivatives","f":4,"d":"A CDS transfers credit risk: protection buyer pays a spread and receives compensation if a defined credit event occurs.","formula":"Premium leg versus protection leg; fair spread equates expected PVs","why":"It allows hedging or trading of issuer credit risk without owning the bond.","caveat":"Basis versus cash bonds, deliverability, recovery and counterparty/collateral matter.","trap_q":"If CDS spread widens, what does that usually indicate?","trap_a":"Market-required compensation for credit protection has increased, typically reflecting higher perceived credit risk or liquidity/risk premium.","es":"CDS transfiere riesgo de crédito; un spread mayor suele reflejar mayor costo de protección.","related":["Credit spread","Default","Recovery"]},{"id":"DER-MARGIN","t":"Initial vs variation margin","cat":"Fixed Income / Derivatives","sub":"Derivatives","f":4,"d":"Initial margin protects against potential future exposure during close-out; variation margin settles current mark-to-market gains and losses.","formula":"VM ≈ change in marked value; IM based on potential future exposure models","why":"Margin reduces counterparty risk in cleared and collateralised derivatives.","caveat":"Margin creates liquidity risk even when an underlying hedge is economically effective.","trap_q":"A hedge is profitable economically but requires large margin cash today. Possible?","trap_a":"Yes. Timing of collateral calls can create liquidity stress before offsetting asset cash flows are realised.","es":"Initial margin cubre exposición potencial futura; variation margin liquida mark-to-market actual.","related":["Clearing","Counterparty risk","Liquidity"]},{"id":"GM-BIDASK","t":"Bid–ask spread","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"Market microstructure","f":4,"d":"Bid is the price a dealer will pay; ask is the price at which it will sell; the difference is the bid–ask spread.","formula":"Spread = Ask − Bid","why":"The spread compensates market makers for inventory, adverse-selection and operating/liquidity risk.","caveat":"Quoted spread is only one part of execution cost; market impact and depth also matter.","trap_q":"In stressed markets, why do spreads often widen?","trap_a":"Liquidity falls, inventory risk and uncertainty rise, so dealers demand more compensation to intermediate.","es":"Bid-ask spread es parte del costo de liquidez y suele ampliarse cuando aumenta incertidumbre.","related":["Liquidity","Market making","Order book"]},{"id":"GM-MAKING","t":"Market making","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"Market microstructure","f":4,"d":"Market makers continuously quote or facilitate buy/sell prices and manage resulting inventory risk.","formula":"P&L comes from spread capture plus/minus inventory and hedging P&L","why":"They provide immediacy and liquidity to clients and markets.","caveat":"They bear adverse-selection and inventory risk and may widen prices or reduce size in volatile conditions.","trap_q":"Why can a dealer lose money despite earning bid–ask spread?","trap_a":"Inventory can move against the dealer or hedging costs can exceed spread income.","es":"Market making gana spread por intermediar, pero asume inventory y hedging risk.","related":["Bid-ask","Liquidity","Hedging"]},{"id":"GM-LIQ","t":"Market liquidity","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"Market microstructure","f":5,"d":"Liquidity is the ability to transact meaningful size quickly with limited price impact and low transaction cost.","formula":"No single formula; proxies include spreads, depth and price impact","why":"Liquidity affects pricing, funding, risk management and the feasibility of exiting positions.","caveat":"High trading volume alone does not guarantee depth during stress.","trap_q":"Can a security be liquid in normal markets and illiquid in stress?","trap_a":"Yes. Liquidity is state-dependent; dealer balance sheets and risk appetite can disappear quickly.","es":"Liquidez significa poder operar tamaño con poco impacto; puede desaparecer justo cuando más se necesita.","related":["Bid-ask","Depth","Funding liquidity"]},{"id":"GM-RISKON","t":"Risk-on / risk-off","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"Cross-asset","f":3,"d":"Risk-on/risk-off describes broad shifts in investor appetite toward or away from risky assets.","formula":"No fixed formula","why":"It is a useful shorthand for correlated cross-asset moves during major macro shocks.","caveat":"It is not a law: asset reactions depend on shock type, positioning, valuation and policy response.","trap_q":"Does risk-off always mean USD strengthens?","trap_a":"No. USD often benefits from liquidity demand and safe-haven flows, but the source of the shock and relative policy expectations can produce different outcomes.","es":"Risk-on/off es un shorthand, no una regla mecánica; la reacción depende del tipo de shock y posicionamiento.","related":["Safe haven","Credit spreads","Equities"]},{"id":"GM-CARRY","t":"Carry trade","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"FX / rates","f":4,"d":"A carry trade seeks return from holding a higher-yielding asset or currency funded with a lower-yielding one, subject to price and FX risk.","formula":"Expected carry ≈ yield differential ± forward/hedging effects","why":"Carry can be attractive in stable regimes and is central to FX and rates relative-value thinking.","caveat":"Small steady carry can be overwhelmed by sharp risk-off or funding-currency appreciation.","trap_q":"Why can a high-yield currency still depreciate enough to lose money?","trap_a":"Interest differential is compensation, not a free return; macro shocks and risk unwinds can dominate carry.","es":"Carry gana diferencial de tasas, pero una devaluación puede borrar varios meses o años de carry.","related":["FX forward","Risk-off","Interest parity"]},{"id":"GM-ARB","t":"Arbitrage","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"Market concepts","f":4,"d":"Arbitrage exploits inconsistent prices for economically equivalent cash flows to lock in a near-riskless profit after costs.","formula":"Same cash flows should have same price under no-arbitrage","why":"No-arbitrage relationships underpin forwards, swaps and relative pricing.","caveat":"Real trades face funding, execution, balance-sheet, model and counterparty constraints.","trap_q":"A textbook arbitrage yields 5 bps but costs 8 bps to execute. Arbitrage?","trap_a":"No. Once transaction costs are included, there is no locked-in positive profit.","es":"Arbitrage requiere beneficio neto prácticamente sin riesgo; costos y funding pueden eliminar la oportunidad teórica.","related":["Law of one price","Forward pricing","Basis"]},{"id":"MAC-GDP","t":"Real vs nominal GDP","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"Macroeconomics","f":4,"d":"Nominal GDP values output at current prices; real GDP adjusts for changes in the price level to measure volume growth.","formula":"Real growth ≈ nominal growth − inflation, approximately for small rates","why":"Separating real activity from inflation is essential for interpreting growth, rates and earnings.","caveat":"GDP revisions, deflators and composition can make headline growth an incomplete view of the economy.","trap_q":"Nominal GDP grows 7% and inflation is 5%. Rough real growth?","trap_a":"Approximately 2% using the simple subtraction approximation.","es":"GDP nominal mezcla cantidad y precios; GDP real intenta aislar el crecimiento de volumen.","related":["GDP deflator","Inflation","Growth"]},{"id":"MAC-CPI","t":"CPI inflation","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"Inflation","f":5,"d":"CPI measures changes in prices paid by consumers for a representative basket of goods and services.","formula":"Inflation rate = % change in index","why":"Inflation influences real income, central-bank policy, yields and valuation discount rates.","caveat":"Basket weights, housing treatment and base effects mean CPI is not identical to every household’s experience or other inflation measures.","trap_q":"CPI prints 50 bps above consensus. Must bonds sell off?","trap_a":"Not mechanically. A hotter print can raise expected rates and yields, but reaction depends on composition, revisions, positioning and what was already priced.","es":"CPI afecta expectativas de política, pero la reacción de mercado depende de sorpresa, composición y pricing previo.","related":["Core CPI","PCE","Rates"]},{"id":"MAC-CORE","t":"Core inflation","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"Inflation","f":4,"d":"Core inflation excludes selected volatile components, commonly food and energy, to gauge underlying price pressure.","formula":"Core index inflation = % change excluding defined volatile categories","why":"Central banks monitor it because transient commodity swings can obscure persistent inflation trends.","caveat":"Excluding volatile items can miss economically important shocks and different core measures behave differently.","trap_q":"Headline falls because oil drops but core stays high. Policy implication?","trap_a":"Underlying inflation pressure may still concern the central bank, so a headline decline alone may not justify rapid easing.","es":"Core inflation busca presión subyacente; headline puede caer por energía sin que la inflación persistente desaparezca.","related":["Headline inflation","PCE","Monetary policy"]},{"id":"MAC-PCE","t":"PCE inflation","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"Inflation","f":4,"d":"PCE price index measures US consumer prices using broader expenditure data and chain-weighting; the Fed closely monitors core PCE.","formula":"% change in PCE price index","why":"It differs from CPI in weights, scope and methodology and is central to US monetary-policy analysis.","caveat":"It is revised and can diverge from CPI, so interview answers should not treat the measures as interchangeable.","trap_q":"Why might CPI and PCE show different inflation rates?","trap_a":"They use different weights, coverage and formulas; healthcare and substitution effects are notable differences.","es":"PCE y CPI no son iguales; cambian pesos, cobertura y metodología.","related":["CPI","Core PCE","Federal Reserve"]},{"id":"MAC-NFP","t":"Non-farm payrolls","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"Labour market","f":4,"d":"NFP measures monthly change in US payroll employment excluding farm workers and certain other categories.","formula":"Headline payroll change plus revisions, unemployment and wages form the broader report","why":"It is a high-frequency signal of labour demand and can materially affect rate expectations.","caveat":"Revisions are common and one month can be noisy; participation and hours matter too.","trap_q":"NFP is strong but unemployment rises. Contradiction?","trap_a":"Not necessarily. Payroll and household surveys differ, and labour-force participation can rise enough to increase unemployment despite job gains.","es":"NFP y unemployment pueden divergir porque vienen de encuestas distintas y cambia la participación laboral.","related":["Unemployment","Wage growth","Participation"]},{"id":"MAC-UNEMP","t":"Unemployment rate","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"Labour market","f":4,"d":"The unemployment rate is unemployed people actively seeking work as a share of the labour force.","formula":"Unemployment rate = Unemployed / Labour Force","why":"It measures labour-market slack and influences wage/inflation and policy expectations.","caveat":"It can fall because workers leave the labour force, so participation must be considered.","trap_q":"Unemployment falls while employment also falls. Possible?","trap_a":"Yes. If labour-force participation falls enough, fewer people are counted as unemployed and the rate can decline.","es":"La tasa puede bajar por menor participación, no solo porque más personas consigan trabajo.","related":["Participation","NFP","NAIRU"]},{"id":"MAC-OUTPUT","t":"Output gap","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"Macroeconomics","f":3,"d":"The output gap is the difference between actual output and estimated potential output.","formula":"Output Gap = Actual GDP − Potential GDP, often as % of potential","why":"Positive gaps can signal demand pressure and inflation risk; negative gaps indicate slack.","caveat":"Potential output is unobservable and estimates are revised substantially.","trap_q":"Can policymakers know the output gap precisely in real time?","trap_a":"No. Potential output is estimated, making the gap highly uncertain and revision-prone.","es":"Output gap compara producción real con potencial, pero el potencial no se observa directamente.","related":["Potential GDP","Inflation","Business cycle"]},{"id":"MAC-PHILLIPS","t":"Phillips curve","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"Macroeconomics","f":3,"d":"The Phillips-curve concept links labour-market slack and inflation pressure, especially through wages and expectations, but the relationship is unstable.","formula":"No fixed universal equation","why":"It provides intuition for why tight labour markets can contribute to inflation.","caveat":"Global supply shocks, expectations, productivity and structural change can weaken the observed relationship.","trap_q":"Does low unemployment guarantee high inflation?","trap_a":"No. The relationship is conditional and can shift; productivity or anchored expectations can offset wage pressure.","es":"Phillips curve es una relación económica útil, no una ley estable entre desempleo e inflación.","related":["NAIRU","Wages","Inflation expectations"]},{"id":"MAC-CYCLE","t":"Business cycle","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"Macroeconomics","f":4,"d":"The business cycle describes expansions and contractions in economic activity around long-run growth.","formula":"No single formula","why":"Cycle position affects earnings, credit, rates, commodities and sector performance.","caveat":"Cycles differ in cause and duration; labels such as early/late cycle are simplifications.","trap_q":"Why can equities rally during weak economic data?","trap_a":"Markets discount the future; weak data may already be priced or may increase expected policy easing enough to support valuations.","es":"Los mercados descuentan futuro, por eso datos débiles no implican automáticamente caída de equities.","related":["Recession","Soft landing","Monetary policy"]},{"id":"MAC-STAG","t":"Stagflation","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"Macroeconomics","f":4,"d":"Stagflation combines weak or stagnant real growth with elevated inflation.","formula":"Low growth + high inflation","why":"It is difficult for policymakers because tightening inflation can worsen growth while easing can sustain inflation.","caveat":"Definitions are qualitative and conditions can vary by severity and duration.","trap_q":"Why is stagflation challenging for a traditional 60/40 portfolio?","trap_a":"Inflation can pressure bond prices through higher yields while weak growth can hurt equities simultaneously.","es":"Stagflation combina crecimiento débil e inflación alta, complicando política y diversificación tradicional.","related":["Inflation","Growth","Rates"]},{"id":"MP-FEDFUNDS","t":"Federal funds rate","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"Monetary policy","f":5,"d":"The federal funds rate is the overnight rate on unsecured reserve balances traded between eligible US depository institutions; the Fed targets a range for this rate.","formula":"Policy implementation uses administered rates and reserve-market tools to keep overnight rates within target range","why":"It anchors short-term USD rates and transmits monetary policy into broader financial conditions.","caveat":"The Fed does not literally set every market interest rate; transmission depends on expectations and market conditions.","trap_q":"What exactly are Fed Funds?","trap_a":"Overnight unsecured lending of reserve balances between eligible institutions; the FOMC sets a target range and the Fed implements policy so market rates trade near it.","es":"Fed Funds es un mercado overnight de reservas; la Fed fija un rango objetivo, no cada tasa de la economía.","related":["FOMC","IORB","SOFR"]},{"id":"MP-IORB","t":"Interest on reserve balances","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"Monetary policy","f":4,"d":"IORB is the rate the Federal Reserve pays eligible banks on reserve balances held at the Fed.","formula":"Administered rate supporting floor-system implementation","why":"It helps anchor overnight rates because banks generally will not lend reserves much below what they can earn risk-free at the Fed, subject to frictions.","caveat":"Not every money-market participant can earn IORB, so other facilities also matter.","trap_q":"Why can Fed Funds trade slightly below IORB?","trap_a":"Some lenders in Fed Funds cannot earn IORB directly and may lend to banks at a slightly lower rate.","es":"IORB ayuda a anclar tasas overnight porque bancos pueden ganar esa tasa sobre reservas.","related":["Fed Funds","ON RRP","Reserves"]},{"id":"MP-SOFR","t":"SOFR","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"Monetary policy","f":5,"d":"SOFR is a broad measure of overnight borrowing cost collateralised by US Treasury securities in the repo market.","formula":"Volume-weighted overnight secured financing rate","why":"It is the principal USD reference rate replacing LIBOR for many derivatives and loans.","caveat":"SOFR is secured and overnight, so it differs economically from unsecured term bank credit risk.","trap_q":"Why is SOFR generally different from Fed Funds?","trap_a":"SOFR is secured by Treasuries and based on repo transactions; Fed Funds is unsecured reserve lending between eligible institutions.","es":"SOFR es una tasa overnight secured por Treasuries; Fed Funds es unsecured reserves lending.","related":["Repo","Fed Funds","Term SOFR"]},{"id":"MP-REPO","t":"Repo","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"Money markets","f":5,"d":"A repo is economically a secured short-term loan: one party sells securities and agrees to repurchase them later at a higher price.","formula":"Repo rate derives from repurchase price difference over time","why":"Repo is core to dealer funding, Treasury-market liquidity and monetary-policy implementation.","caveat":"Collateral quality, haircuts and balance-sheet constraints can drive rates and liquidity.","trap_q":"Why can repo rates spike even with safe Treasury collateral?","trap_a":"Cash scarcity, dealer balance-sheet constraints, settlement flows or collateral dynamics can create funding pressure.","es":"Repo es préstamo secured disfrazado como venta y recompra; es central para funding de mercados.","related":["SOFR","Collateral","Haircut"]},{"id":"MP-QE","t":"Quantitative easing","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"Monetary policy","f":5,"d":"QE is large-scale central-bank asset purchasing intended to ease financial conditions when policy-rate tools are constrained or additional accommodation is desired.","formula":"Central bank assets ↑; reserve balances generally ↑ as purchases settle","why":"QE can compress term premia, improve liquidity and signal policy accommodation.","caveat":"Effects depend on expectations, asset scarcity, banking transmission and fiscal context; reserve creation does not mechanically equal bank lending.","trap_q":"Does QE automatically cause proportional CPI inflation?","trap_a":"No. Transmission depends on credit demand, supply conditions, expectations, fiscal policy and broader money/velocity dynamics.","es":"QE compra activos y expande balance/reservas; no significa automáticamente inflación proporcional ni préstamos bancarios.","related":["QT","Term premium","Reserves"]},{"id":"MP-QT","t":"Quantitative tightening","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"Monetary policy","f":4,"d":"QT reduces the central bank balance sheet, typically by allowing securities to mature without full reinvestment or through sales.","formula":"Central bank asset holdings ↓ over time","why":"It withdraws some balance-sheet accommodation and can affect reserves, term premia and market liquidity.","caveat":"The market impact is not a simple mirror image of QE and depends on reserve abundance and Treasury issuance.","trap_q":"Does QT mean the central bank literally destroys the same amount of bank credit?","trap_a":"No. It changes central-bank assets/reserves; private bank lending depends on separate capital, funding and demand conditions.","es":"QT reduce balance del central bank, pero no es simplemente “destruir préstamos” uno por uno.","related":["QE","Reserves","Liquidity"]},{"id":"MP-TRANS","t":"Monetary transmission","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"Monetary policy","f":5,"d":"Monetary transmission is the chain from policy settings through money-market rates, yield curves, credit, FX, asset prices, spending, employment and inflation.","formula":"Policy rate → market rates/expectations → financial conditions → demand → inflation","why":"It explains why a central-bank decision affects the real economy with lags and through multiple channels.","caveat":"Transmission varies by shock, banking system, fixed/floating debt mix, fiscal policy and expectations.","trap_q":"Walk through a Fed hike.","trap_a":"Front-end rates usually rise, broader yields/credit and FX may adjust, borrowing becomes more expensive, demand tends to slow and inflation pressure may ease with lags—but responses depend on expectations and the reason for the hike.","es":"La subida de tasas se transmite por mercados, crédito, FX y demanda con rezagos; no es una cadena mecánica idéntica cada vez.","related":["Fed Funds","Yield curve","Financial conditions"]},{"id":"MP-SURPRISE","t":"Macro surprise versus expectations","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"Market reaction","f":5,"d":"Markets react primarily to new information relative to what was expected and priced, not to the absolute level of a data release alone.","formula":"Surprise = Actual − Consensus, interpreted versus positioning/pricing","why":"This explains why “bad” data can trigger rallies and “good” data can trigger sell-offs.","caveat":"Consensus is imperfect and positioning, revisions and details can dominate the headline surprise.","trap_q":"Inflation falls from 4% to 3.5% but consensus was 3.2%. Dovish or hawkish surprise?","trap_a":"Hawkish relative to expectations: inflation is lower in absolute terms but hotter than priced.","es":"El mercado compara dato con expectativa, no solo con el dato anterior.","related":["CPI","Positioning","Rates"]},{"id":"MP-FX","t":"Rates and FX","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"FX","f":5,"d":"FX reflects relative expected returns, policy paths, growth, risk premia, trade flows and positioning between two currencies.","formula":"Covered forward pricing reflects interest-rate differential; spot reaction is broader","why":"Higher relative rates can support a currency by raising expected return, all else equal.","caveat":"If higher rates reflect severe inflation or crisis risk, the currency can weaken; expectations matter more than static rate levels.","trap_q":"Why might a rate cut strengthen a currency?","trap_a":"If the cut is smaller than expected, removes recession risk, or improves growth/risk sentiment enough to attract capital, the currency can strengthen.","es":"FX depende de tasas relativas y expectativas, pero también de growth, risk premium y qué estaba priced.","related":["Carry","Interest parity","Monetary policy"]},{"id":"MP-INFLX","t":"Inflation surprise cross-asset","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"Market reaction","f":5,"d":"An upside inflation surprise can shift expected policy rates, real yields, curves, equities, FX, commodities and credit, but the direction is conditional on expectations and shock composition.","formula":"No fixed formula; trace policy and discount-rate channels","why":"Scenario analysis tests whether you can connect macro data to multiple asset classes coherently.","caveat":"Supply-driven versus demand-driven inflation, positioning and starting valuations can produce different reactions.","trap_q":"CPI is 50 bps above consensus. Give a balanced first-pass market map.","trap_a":"Front-end yields may rise and cuts price out; bonds may fall; growth equities may face higher discount rates; USD may strengthen on relative-rate expectations; credit may widen if growth risk rises—but all are conditional on why inflation surprised and prior positioning.","es":"Un surprise inflacionario suele mover expectativas de tasas primero, pero la reacción final depende de causa, magnitud y pricing previo.","related":["CPI","Yield curve","FX"]},{"id":"MAC-FISC","t":"Fiscal deficits and yields","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"Fiscal policy","f":4,"d":"Fiscal deficits occur when government spending exceeds revenue and must be financed through borrowing, cash balances or money creation depending on the institutional setting.","formula":"Deficit = Spending − Revenue","why":"Large deficits can support demand but may increase debt supply and term premia, especially near capacity.","caveat":"Yield effects depend on central-bank policy, private saving, growth impact and global demand for government bonds.","trap_q":"Do larger deficits always raise bond yields?","trap_a":"No. They can, through supply/inflation channels, but recessions or safe-haven demand may simultaneously push yields lower.","es":"Déficits pueden impulsar demanda y aumentar oferta de deuda, pero el efecto en yields depende del contexto macro y policy.","related":["Public debt","Term premium","Crowding out"]},{"id":"MAC-BOP","t":"Balance of payments","cat":"Global Markets / Macro","sub":"International economics","f":3,"d":"The balance of payments records a country’s transactions with the rest of the world across current, capital and financial accounts.","formula":"Current account + capital/financial flows + errors ≈ 0 in accounting terms","why":"It links trade/income balances with financing flows and FX dynamics.","caveat":"A current-account deficit is not automatically bad if financed sustainably by productive capital inflows.","trap_q":"Country runs a current-account deficit. Must its currency weaken?","trap_a":"No. Strong capital inflows can finance the deficit and support the currency; valuation and expectations also matter.","es":"BOP conecta current account con flujos financieros; un déficit externo no implica devaluación automática.","related":["Current account","Capital flows","FX"]},{"id":"BK-NIM","t":"Net interest margin","cat":"Banking / Capital Markets","sub":"Banks","f":5,"d":"NIM measures net interest income relative to average interest-earning assets.","formula":"NIM = Net Interest Income / Average Earning Assets","why":"It is a core bank profitability metric driven by asset yields, funding costs, deposit beta, mix and hedging.","caveat":"A steeper curve does not always raise NIM; deposit repricing, hedge books and loan mix matter.","trap_q":"Policy rates rise. Does bank NIM always rise?","trap_a":"No. Asset yields may reprice upward, but deposit/funding costs can rise faster and hedges or asset mix can offset the benefit.","es":"NIM mide spread neto de intereses sobre activos productivos; depende de repricing de activos y funding.","related":["Deposit beta","Yield curve","Banks"]},{"id":"BK-CET1","t":"CET1 ratio","cat":"Banking / Capital Markets","sub":"Banks","f":5,"d":"CET1 ratio compares highest-quality common equity regulatory capital with risk-weighted assets.","formula":"CET1 Ratio = CET1 Capital / RWA","why":"It measures a bank’s core loss-absorbing capital relative to risk-weighted exposures and is central to regulatory capacity.","caveat":"RWA uses regulatory risk weights rather than simple asset totals; ratio changes can come from numerator or denominator.","trap_q":"Can CET1 ratio rise without issuing equity or retaining profit?","trap_a":"Yes. RWA can fall through asset mix changes, disposals or model/regulatory changes.","es":"CET1 ratio mide capital de mayor calidad sobre activos ponderados por riesgo, no sobre activos totales.","related":["RWA","Leverage ratio","Bank capital"]},{"id":"BK-LCR","t":"Liquidity coverage ratio","cat":"Banking / Capital Markets","sub":"Banks","f":4,"d":"LCR requires banks to hold sufficient high-quality liquid assets to cover stressed net cash outflows over 30 days.","formula":"LCR = HQLA / 30-day stressed net cash outflows","why":"It targets short-term liquidity resilience under stress.","caveat":"Regulatory assumptions may differ from actual depositor behaviour and LCR is not a complete funding-risk measure.","trap_q":"LCR above 100% means a bank cannot face a run?","trap_a":"No. It indicates regulatory liquidity coverage under specified assumptions, not immunity from extreme or faster outflows.","es":"LCR cubre 30 días de salidas estresadas con HQLA; no garantiza ausencia de run.","related":["HQLA","NSFR","Liquidity risk"]},{"id":"BK-NSFR","t":"Net stable funding ratio","cat":"Banking / Capital Markets","sub":"Banks","f":3,"d":"NSFR compares available stable funding with required stable funding over a one-year horizon.","formula":"NSFR = Available Stable Funding / Required Stable Funding","why":"It discourages funding long-lived assets with excessively unstable short-term liabilities.","caveat":"Regulatory weights are standardised and do not capture every idiosyncratic funding risk.","trap_q":"How does NSFR differ from LCR?","trap_a":"LCR is a 30-day stress liquidity metric; NSFR focuses on structural one-year funding stability.","es":"NSFR mira estabilidad estructural de funding a un año; LCR mira liquidez de 30 días.","related":["LCR","Funding","Bank liquidity"]},{"id":"BK-COSTRISK","t":"Cost of risk","cat":"Banking / Capital Markets","sub":"Banks","f":4,"d":"Cost of risk measures credit impairment/provision expense relative to the loan book, often expressed in basis points.","formula":"Cost of Risk ≈ Credit Loss Expense / Average Loans","why":"It shows how much credit deterioration is consuming bank earnings.","caveat":"Provision timing, overlays and write-offs can make one period unrepresentative of through-cycle loss.","trap_q":"NPL ratio is flat but cost of risk spikes. Possible?","trap_a":"Yes. The bank may provision more heavily for expected deterioration, change assumptions or face losses in still-performing exposures.","es":"Cost of risk mide provisiones/pérdidas de crédito sobre préstamos y puede adelantarse al NPL ratio.","related":["NPL","Provision coverage","Expected credit loss"]},{"id":"BK-NPL","t":"NPL ratio","cat":"Banking / Capital Markets","sub":"Banks","f":4,"d":"NPL ratio measures non-performing loans relative to total loans under the applicable definition.","formula":"NPL Ratio = Non-performing Loans / Gross Loans","why":"It is a core measure of asset quality and credit deterioration.","caveat":"Definitions and cure/write-off practices differ across banks and jurisdictions.","trap_q":"Can NPL ratio fall because credit quality worsened?","trap_a":"Potentially, if bad loans are sold or written off faster than new NPL formation; examine flows and coverage.","es":"NPL ratio mide préstamos problemáticos, pero puede bajar por write-offs o ventas, no solo por mejora.","related":["Cost of risk","Coverage ratio","Asset quality"]},{"id":"BK-CIR","t":"Cost-to-income ratio","cat":"Banking / Capital Markets","sub":"Banks","f":4,"d":"Cost-to-income compares operating expenses with operating income and is a common bank efficiency metric.","formula":"Cost-to-Income = Operating Expenses / Operating Income","why":"Lower ratios generally indicate better operating efficiency, subject to growth and investment context.","caveat":"Cutting necessary investment can improve the ratio temporarily while harming future franchise value.","trap_q":"Is a lower cost-to-income ratio always better?","trap_a":"No. A bank may be underinvesting, while a temporarily higher ratio may reflect productive growth investment.","es":"Cost-to-income mide eficiencia bancaria, pero un ratio bajo no siempre significa mejor estrategia.","related":["Efficiency","Operating expenses","Bank profitability"]},{"id":"BK-VAL","t":"Bank valuation","cat":"Banking / Capital Markets","sub":"Banks","f":5,"d":"Banks are often valued using P/E, P/B or P/TBV, DDM or excess-return approaches because debt/funding is integral to operations and EBITDA is not economically meaningful in the same way as for industrial companies.","formula":"P/TBV = Price / Tangible Book Value per share","why":"Book equity and returns on that equity are central because capital constrains bank growth and distributions.","caveat":"Book quality, asset marks, credit losses and required capital can make simple P/B comparisons misleading.","trap_q":"Why is EV/EBITDA generally poor for banks?","trap_a":"Interest is operating revenue/cost, debt is raw material rather than purely financing, and regulatory capital—not enterprise leverage—drives capacity.","es":"Bancos suelen valorarse con P/E, P/TBV y DDM porque interest y funding son parte del negocio operativo.","related":["P/TBV","ROTE","CET1"]},{"id":"ECM-IPO","t":"IPO","cat":"Banking / Capital Markets","sub":"ECM","f":4,"d":"An IPO is the first public sale/listing of a company’s shares, involving preparation, regulatory disclosure, marketing, bookbuilding and pricing.","formula":"Primary proceeds = new shares × offer price; secondary proceeds go to selling holders","why":"It raises capital, creates liquidity and establishes a public market valuation.","caveat":"Pricing balances issuer proceeds with aftermarket performance and execution certainty; lockups and dilution matter.","trap_q":"Primary vs secondary shares in an IPO?","trap_a":"Primary shares are newly issued and raise cash for the company; secondary shares are sold by existing shareholders and proceeds go to them.","es":"IPO puede incluir primary shares que financian a la empresa y secondary shares que monetizan accionistas existentes.","related":["Bookbuilding","Underwriting","Dilution"]},{"id":"ECM-RIGHTS","t":"Rights issue","cat":"Banking / Capital Markets","sub":"ECM","f":3,"d":"A rights issue offers existing shareholders the right to buy new shares, usually pro rata and often at a discount.","formula":"Theoretical ex-rights price depends on old shares, new shares and subscription price","why":"It raises equity while giving existing holders an opportunity to preserve ownership percentage.","caveat":"Shareholders who do not participate can be diluted unless rights are tradable and monetised.","trap_q":"Why price a rights issue at a discount?","trap_a":"To encourage take-up and provide a buffer against market-price volatility during the offer period.","es":"Rights issue permite a accionistas mantener su porcentaje si participan; no participar puede diluirlos.","related":["Dilution","TERP","Equity issuance"]},{"id":"ECM-BOOK","t":"Bookbuilding","cat":"Banking / Capital Markets","sub":"ECM / DCM","f":4,"d":"Bookbuilding collects investor orders across prices and sizes to determine demand, allocation and final pricing for an issuance.","formula":"Order book maps demand versus price/spread","why":"It helps issuers balance price, size, investor quality and aftermarket stability.","caveat":"Books can be inflated by price-insensitive or duplicated indications; headline oversubscription is not the whole story.","trap_q":"A deal is 10x oversubscribed. Does that prove it was underpriced?","trap_a":"No. Order quality, sensitivity, duplicates and strategic allocation matter; strong demand can coexist with fair pricing.","es":"Bookbuilding descubre demanda, pero oversubscription bruta no equivale automáticamente a underpricing.","related":["IPO","Bond issuance","Allocation"]},{"id":"ECM-GREEN","t":"Greenshoe option","cat":"Banking / Capital Markets","sub":"ECM","f":3,"d":"A greenshoe gives underwriters an over-allotment option, typically allowing additional shares to be purchased from the issuer or sellers to facilitate stabilisation.","formula":"Often up to a specified % of base offering subject to rules","why":"It helps manage excess demand and aftermarket stabilisation around an equity offering.","caveat":"Mechanics and stabilisation rules are regulated and jurisdiction-specific.","trap_q":"Why can underwriters initially sell more shares than the base deal size?","trap_a":"The over-allotment creates a short position that can be covered through the greenshoe or market purchases, supporting stabilisation mechanics.","es":"Greenshoe permite over-allotment y ayuda a estabilizar el precio post-IPO dentro de reglas específicas.","related":["IPO","Stabilisation","Underwriting"]},{"id":"DCM-ISSUE","t":"Bond issuance process","cat":"Banking / Capital Markets","sub":"DCM","f":4,"d":"A bond issuance typically involves mandate, structuring, documentation, ratings/marketing, initial price thoughts, bookbuilding, pricing, allocation and settlement.","formula":"Coupon/yield set relative to benchmark curve plus spread","why":"It connects issuer funding needs with investor demand and market conditions.","caveat":"Execution depends on rates volatility, credit spread, tenor, covenants and investor technicals.","trap_q":"Why might an issuer delay a bond deal after announcing it?","trap_a":"Market volatility or widening spreads may make pricing unattractive or execution uncertain.","es":"DCM issuance depende de benchmark yield más credit spread y puede retrasarse si la ventana de mercado se deteriora.","related":["Credit spread","Bookbuilding","Syndicate"]},{"id":"DCM-REFI","t":"Refinancing & maturity wall","cat":"Banking / Capital Markets","sub":"DCM","f":4,"d":"Refinancing replaces maturing debt with new funding; a maturity wall is a concentration of debt coming due in a limited period.","formula":"Refinancing need = maturities − available cash/internal funding","why":"It is a key credit risk because otherwise solvent companies can face stress if markets close when large debt matures.","caveat":"Access depends on rates, spreads, covenants, ratings and market windows.","trap_q":"Can a profitable company default because of a maturity wall?","trap_a":"Yes. Liquidity/refinancing failure can cause default even if the underlying business is profitable.","es":"Maturity wall concentra vencimientos; una empresa rentable puede fallar si no logra refinanciar a tiempo.","related":["Liquidity","Refinancing risk","Credit"]},{"id":"EQ-BMODEL","t":"Business model analysis","cat":"Banking / Capital Markets","sub":"Equity analysis","f":4,"d":"Business-model analysis identifies how a company creates value: customers, product, pricing, revenue model, cost structure, capital intensity and competitive position.","formula":"Revenue drivers − cost drivers − reinvestment = economics","why":"It is the foundation for forecasting and valuation because numbers must map to operating reality.","caveat":"A neat financial model can be wrong if it lacks causal operating drivers.","trap_q":"What would you ask before forecasting revenue?","trap_a":"Who pays, for what, how price and volume are set, retention/repeat behaviour, capacity, competition and the relevant market size.","es":"Antes de modelar números hay que entender cómo gana dinero el negocio: precio, volumen, costos, capital y competencia.","related":["Revenue drivers","Margins","Competitive advantage"]},{"id":"EQ-MOAT","t":"Competitive advantage / moat","cat":"Banking / Capital Markets","sub":"Equity analysis","f":4,"d":"A competitive advantage is a durable feature that allows superior economics, such as switching costs, network effects, cost advantage, brand or scarce assets.","formula":"Moat should manifest in durable pricing, margins, growth or ROIC","why":"It influences forecast durability, terminal assumptions and justified valuation multiples.","caveat":"Claims of “brand” or “scale” are not sufficient unless they produce measurable economic outcomes and survive competition.","trap_q":"High current ROIC proves a moat?","trap_a":"No. High ROIC may be cyclical or temporary; durability, reinvestment opportunities and competitive response matter.","es":"Un moat debe reflejarse en retornos sostenibles y resistencia competitiva, no solo en una narrativa.","related":["ROIC","Pricing power","Industry structure"]}];
